var PLACES=[
/* ── MAKKAH · The Haram ── */
{id:'haram',ar:'المسجد الحرام',ll:[21.4225,39.8262],city:'makkah',g:'haram',i:'🕋',n:'Masjid al-Haram & the Kaaba',q:'Masjid al-Haram Makkah',d:'The House of Allah, qibla of the world, site of your tawaf. "The first House established for mankind" (Quran 3:96); every prayer here is worth 100,000 (Ibn Majah 1406).',tip:'Landmarks inside: Hajar al-Aswad, Maqam Ibrahim, Hijr Isma’il, the Multazam & the Yamani corner.'},
{id:'hijr',ar:'المسجد الحرام',ll:[21.4225,39.8262],city:'makkah',g:'haram',i:'🌙',n:'Hijr Isma’il & Maqam Ibrahim',q:'Masjid al-Haram Makkah',d:'The semicircle beside the Kaaba was part of Ibrahim’s original building — praying inside it counts as praying inside the Kaaba (Tirmidhi 876). Maqam Ibrahim is the stone he stood on while raising the walls (Bukhari 3364; Quran 2:125).',tip:'Try the Hijr late at night after tawaf; pray your 2 rakahs anywhere behind the Maqam if the area is packed.'},
{id:'zamzam',ar:'المسجد الحرام',ll:[21.4225,39.8262],city:'makkah',g:'haram',i:'💧',n:'The Well of Zamzam',q:'Zamzam Well Makkah',d:'Gushed out where an angel dug the ground with his heel — at the spot where baby Isma’il had been left — after Hajar’s seventh run between the hills (Bukhari 3364). "Zamzam water is for whatever it is drunk for" (Ibn Majah 3062).',tip:'The well itself is now beneath the mataf; chilled and room-temperature dispensers are on every floor. Face the qibla and make dua as you drink.'},
{id:'safa',ar:'المسجد الحرام',ll:[21.4234,39.8278],city:'makkah',g:'haram',i:'⛰️',n:'Safa & Marwah (the Mas’a)',q:'Safa and Marwah Makkah',d:'"Indeed Safa and Marwah are among the symbols of Allah" (Quran 2:158). The two hills of Hajar’s seven runs, now an air-conditioned 394 m gallery inside the mosque.',tip:'Wheelchair lanes run down the middle; the green lights mark where men jog. Begin at Safa, finish at Marwah.'},
/* ── MAKKAH · Ziyarah ── */
{id:'hira',ar:'جبل النور – غار حراء',tags:['🥾 45–90 min climb','👁️ View from below is fine'],climb:true,ll:[21.4575,39.8595],city:'makkah',g:'ziyarah',i:'⛰️',n:'Jabal an-Nour & the Cave of Hira',q:'Jabal al-Nour Makkah',d:'Where the first revelation "Iqra" descended upon the Prophet ﷺ (Bukhari 3).',tip:'A steep 45–90 min climb — go after Fajr, take water. Viewing from below also suffices; see the Revelation Exhibition at its foot.'},
{id:'thawr',ar:'جبل ثور',tags:['🥾 Hard climb','👁️ Usually viewed from road'],climb:true,ll:[21.3771,39.8498],city:'makkah',g:'ziyarah',i:'🏔️',n:'Jabal Thawr',q:'Jabal Thawr Makkah',d:'The cave where the Prophet ﷺ and Abu Bakr hid for three nights during the Hijrah — "Do not grieve; indeed Allah is with us" (Quran 9:40).',tip:'A tougher climb than Hira; most pilgrims view it from the road on a ziyarah tour.'},
{id:'birth',ar:'مكتبة مكة المكرمة (مولد النبي ﷺ)',ll:[21.423,39.83],city:'makkah',g:'ziyarah',i:'🏠',n:'Birthplace of the Prophet ﷺ (Makkah Library)',q:'Makkah Al Mukarramah Library',d:'By long-standing tradition the site of the house of Aminah where the Prophet ﷺ was born in the Year of the Elephant, in the Shi’b Ali quarter. Today it houses a library.',tip:'A few minutes’ walk from the Safa side of the Haram. Viewed from outside — it is not a place of worship; reflect, don’t ritualise.'},
{id:'abuqubays',ar:'جبل أبي قبيس',noroute:1,ll:[21.4225,39.83],city:'makkah',g:'ziyarah',i:'🏔️',n:'Jabal Abu Qubays',q:'Jabal Abu Qubais Makkah',d:'The mountain rising directly east of the Haram, held by tradition to be the first mountain placed on earth and the place the Black Stone was safeguarded during the flood.',tip:'Now crowned by a royal palace; visible from the Safa end of the mataf — no visit needed.'},
{id:'rayah',ar:'مسجد الراية',noroute:1,city:'makkah',g:'ziyarah',i:'🚩',n:'Site of Masjid ar-Rayah (demolished)',q:'Masjid Al Rayah Makkah',d:'"The Mosque of the Banner" — by tradition the spot where the Prophet ﷺ planted his standard as he entered Makkah at the Conquest of 8 AH, forgiving the city that had expelled him.',tip:'No longer standing — it was in the old al-Jawdariyyah quarter just north of the Haram and was demolished around 1431–1433 AH for the northern expansion of the Haram courtyards. Nothing remains to visit.'},
{id:'taneem',ar:'مسجد عائشة – التنعيم',ll:[21.4693,39.8016],city:'makkah',g:'ziyarah',i:'🕌',n:'Masjid Aisha (Tan’eem)',q:'Masjid Aisha Taneem Makkah',d:'The nearest boundary of the Haram — where the Prophet ﷺ sent Aishah (RA) to take ihram for her Umrah (Bukhari 1784). Take ihram here for an additional Umrah.',tip:'10–15 min taxi; a Makkah Bus route also serves Tan’eem — check the Makkah Bus app for the current route and fare. Showers and ihram shops on site.'},
{id:'jiranah',ar:'مسجد الجعرانة',ll:[21.5682,39.9511],city:'makkah',g:'ziyarah',i:'🕌',n:'Masjid al-Ji’ranah',q:'Masjid Al Jiranah Makkah',d:'Where the Prophet ﷺ assumed ihram for his Umrah after the Battle of Hunayn (Bukhari 1778) — one of his four Umrahs. A miqat for those already in Makkah.',tip:'~25 km north-east; far calmer than Tan’eem for a second Umrah.'},
{id:'hudaybiyyah',ar:'مسجد الحديبية (الشميسي)',ll:[21.4327,39.6452],city:'makkah',g:'ziyarah',i:'🌳',n:'Masjid al-Hudaybiyyah',q:'Masjid Al Hudaibiyah Makkah',d:'Site of the Treaty of Hudaybiyyah (6 AH) and the Pledge under the Tree — "Allah was pleased with the believers when they pledged allegiance to you under the tree" (Quran 48:18). Also a miqat on the western Haram boundary.',tip:'~25 km west on the Jeddah road; the old mosque ruins stand beside the modern one.'},
{id:'mualla',ar:'مقبرة المعلاة',tags:['👤 Men enter','🕐 Limited hours','🌸 Sisters: salam from outside'],ll:[21.437,39.8292],city:'makkah',g:'ziyarah',i:'🌿',n:'Jannat al-Mu’alla',q:'Jannat al-Mualla Makkah',d:'Makkah’s ancient cemetery — resting place of Khadijah (RA), the Prophet’s ﷺ beloved first wife, and of his grandfather Abdul-Muttalib and uncle Abu Talib.',tip:'Give salam to the deceased; visiting hours are limited and entry is for men.'},
{id:'jinn',ar:'مسجد الجن',ll:[21.431,39.8288],city:'makkah',g:'ziyarah',i:'🕌',n:'Masjid al-Jinn',q:'Masjid al-Jinn Makkah',d:'By tradition the place where a group of jinn came to the Prophet ﷺ, heard him recite the Quran and pledged allegiance (cf. Muslim 450) — hence its other name, the Mosque of Allegiance. Surah al-Jinn (72) tells of jinn believing on hearing the Quran.',tip:'A short walk north of the Haram, near Jannat al-Mu’alla.'},
/* ── MAKKAH · Hajj sites ── */
{id:'arafat',ar:'عرفات – جبل الرحمة',ll:[21.3549,39.9841],city:'makkah',g:'hajj',i:'🌄',n:'Mount Arafat & Jabal ar-Rahmah',q:'Jabal ar-Rahmah Arafat',d:'The plain of the Farewell Sermon where Hajj reaches its peak — "Hajj is Arafah" (Tirmidhi 889). Standing here on 9 Dhul-Hijjah is the heart of Hajj.',tip:'Combine Arafat, Muzdalifah & Mina in one half-day ziyarah taxi tour (agree the price first).'},
{id:'namirah',ar:'مسجد نمرة',tags:['📅 Arafah day only'],noroute:1,ll:[21.353,39.9663],city:'makkah',g:'hajj',i:'🕌',n:'Masjid Namirah (Arafat)',q:'Masjid Namirah Arafat',d:'The Prophet ﷺ had his tent pitched at Namirah on the day of Arafah, then addressed the people and prayed Dhuhr and Asr combined (Muslim 1218). One of the largest mosques in the world.',tip:'Opens only on the day of Arafah; outside Hajj you view it from the road.'},
{id:'muzdalifah',ar:'مزدلفة',ll:[21.3833,39.9366],city:'makkah',g:'hajj',i:'🌌',n:'Muzdalifah',q:'Muzdalifah',d:'Where Hajj pilgrims sleep under the open sky between Arafat and Mina — named in the Quran as al-Mash’ar al-Haram (2:198).',tip:'Drive-through on a ziyarah tour; picture more than 1.5 million people sleeping here in one night.'},
{id:'mashar',ar:'مسجد المشعر الحرام',ll:[21.3861,39.9122],city:'makkah',g:'hajj',i:'🕌',n:'Masjid al-Mash’ar al-Haram',q:'Masjid Al Mashar Al Haram Muzdalifah',d:'Marks the spot where the Prophet ﷺ stood at Muzdalifah making dua until the sky brightened — "remember Allah at al-Mash’ar al-Haram" (Quran 2:198).',tip:'Between Arafat and Mina on the pilgrim road.'},
{id:'mina',ar:'منى',ll:[21.4133,39.8933],city:'makkah',g:'hajj',i:'⛺',n:'Mina',q:'Mina Makkah',d:'The tent city of Hajj — 100,000 fireproof tents — and the site of the Jamarat and the sacrifice.',tip:'Quiet outside Hajj season — a moving drive-through with Quran 22:27 in mind.'},
{id:'khayf',ar:'مسجد الخيف',ll:[21.4153,39.8778],city:'makkah',g:'hajj',i:'🕌',n:'Masjid al-Khayf (Mina)',q:'Masjid Al Khaif Mina',d:'The great mosque of Mina where the Prophet ﷺ prayed during his Hajj; narrations reported by at-Tabarani say many prophets before him prayed in this valley.',tip:'Holds about 25,000; it opens only in the Hajj season (8 Dhul-Hijjah, Eid and the days of Tashriq) — outside Hajj you see it from the road.'},
{id:'bayah',ar:'مسجد البيعة',ll:[21.4247,39.8675],noroute:1,city:'makkah',g:'hajj',i:'🤝',n:'Masjid al-Bay’ah (Aqabah)',q:'Masjid Al Bayah Mina',d:'Where the people of Madinah secretly pledged allegiance to the Prophet ﷺ in the two Pledges of Aqabah (621–622 CE) — the moment that opened the door to the Hijrah.',tip:'A small, easily-missed mosque near the Jamarat bridge; built in 144 AH.'},
{id:'jamarat',ar:'الجمرات',ll:[21.4224,39.8735],city:'makkah',g:'hajj',i:'🪨',n:'The Jamarat',q:'Jamarat Bridge Mina',d:'The three jamarat (now long walls) marking where, in a report from Ibn Abbas (RA), Ibrahim (AS) stoned Shaytan when tempted to disobey — re-enacted by every Hajj pilgrim on the Day of Sacrifice (the big jamrah only) and the days of Tashriq.',tip:'The multi-level bridge is an engineering marvel; visit outside Hajj to understand the layout before you ever come for Hajj.'},
/* ── MAKKAH · Museums & culture ── */
{id:'hirac',ar:'حي حراء الثقافي',ll:[21.4571,39.8692],city:'makkah',g:'museum',i:'🏛️',n:'Hira Cultural District & Revelation Exhibition',q:'Hira Cultural District Makkah',d:'A modern museum at the foot of Jabal an-Nour telling the story of wahy (revelation) with immersive exhibits — the context for everything you recite.',tip:'The perfect alternative to the climb for the elderly and children. Book online; evening slots are cooler.'},
{id:'exhibition',ar:'معرض عمارة الحرمين الشريفين',ll:[21.4346,39.7547],city:'makkah',g:'museum',i:'🏛️',n:'Exhibition of the Two Holy Mosques',q:'Exhibition of the Two Holy Mosques Architecture Makkah',d:'Historic Kaaba doors, kiswah textiles, the old Zamzam well-head, Maqam Ibrahim casings and rare artefacts of the Haramain across the centuries.',tip:'In Umm al-Joud on the old Jeddah road, next to the Kiswah complex. Entry has traditionally been free — check hours and entry rules before going.'},
{id:'kiswah',ar:'مجمع الملك عبدالعزيز لكسوة الكعبة المشرفة',ll:[21.4343,39.7564],tags:['🎫 By arrangement/tour'],city:'makkah',g:'museum',i:'🧵',n:'The Kiswah Factory (King Abdulaziz Complex)',q:'Kiswa Factory Makkah',d:'Where the black silk covering of the Kaaba is woven each year and embroidered with about 120 kg of gold thread and 100 kg of silver thread — a craft of honouring the House that is centuries old.',tip:'Visits are by arrangement/tour; the exhibition explains every panel of calligraphy you see on the Kaaba.'},
{id:'makkahmuseum',ar:'متحف مكة – قصر الزاهر',ll:[21.4461,39.8080],city:'makkah',g:'museum',i:'🏛️',n:'Makkah Museum (Al-Zahir Palace)',q:'Makkah Museum Al Zahir Palace',d:'The history of the Hijaz from pre-Islamic times through the prophetic era to the modern Kingdom, in a 1940s palace.',tip:'Confirm it is open before going — it closed during the pandemic and may still be shut. The Exhibition of the Two Holy Mosques is about 6 km away in Umm al-Joud.'},
{id:'clock',ar:'متحف برج الساعة',ll:[21.4187,39.8256],city:'makkah',g:'museum',i:'🕰️',n:'Makkah Clock Tower Museum',q:'Clock Tower Museum Makkah',d:'Astronomy and Islamic timekeeping museum atop the Abraj al-Bait — four floors, then a breathtaking balcony view over the Haram.',tip:'Book in advance; sunset slots have the best views.'},
/* ── MAKKAH · Day trips ── */
{id:'taif',ar:'الطائف – مسجد عبدالله بن عباس',ll:[21.2704,40.4085],city:'makkah',g:'trip',i:'🌹',n:'Ta’if & Masjid Abdullah ibn Abbas',q:'Masjid Abdullah Ibn Abbas Taif',d:'The mountain city that stoned the Prophet ﷺ in the 10th year of his mission — when the Angel of the Mountains offered to bring the two mountains (al-Akhshabayn) down upon those who rejected him, he refused, hoping for believing descendants (Bukhari 3231). Today: cool air, rose farms, and the mosque of Ibn Abbas (RA).',tip:'~1.5 h by road up the escarpment. Wadi Muharram on the way is a miqat if you return in ihram.'},
{id:'jeddah',ar:'جدة التاريخية – البلد',ll:[21.4858,39.1875],city:'makkah',g:'trip',i:'🏘️',n:'Historic Jeddah (Al-Balad)',q:'Al Balad Jeddah',d:'The UNESCO-listed old town that was the sea gate of pilgrims to Makkah for thirteen centuries — coral-stone houses, wooden rawashin balconies, ancient mosques.',tip:'An evening stroll on the way to the airport. A cultural, not religious, visit.'},
/* ── MADINAH · The Prophet’s Mosque & around ── */
{id:'nabawi',ar:'المسجد النبوي',ll:[24.4672,39.6111],city:'madinah',g:'mharam',i:'🕌',n:'Masjid an-Nabawi',q:'Al Masjid an Nabawi Madinah',d:'The Prophet’s ﷺ mosque, built with his own hands — one prayer here is better than a thousand elsewhere, except al-Masjid al-Haram (Bukhari 1190).',tip:'Give salam to the Prophet ﷺ and his two companions with calm and lowered voice.',stop:'G1'},
{id:'rawdah',ck:CK,cks:'rawdah',ar:'المسجد النبوي',tags:['🎫 Free Nusuk permit','🌸 Women’s slots'],ll:[24.4672,39.6111],city:'madinah',g:'mharam',i:'🌹',n:'The Rawdah',q:'Al Masjid an Nabawi Madinah',d:'"Between my house and my minbar is a garden from the gardens of Paradise" (Bukhari 1195).',tip:'Requires a free timed permit from the Nusuk app — slots open only a short time ahead, so check Nusuk as your dates approach and book as soon as they open.'},
{id:'babsalam',ar:'المسجد النبوي – باب السلام',ll:[24.4677,39.6104],city:'madinah',g:'mharam',i:'🚪',n:'Bab as-Salam (Al Salam Gate)',q:'Bab As Salam Al Masjid an Nabawi',d:'The historic western gate of the mosque — for centuries the traditional entrance by which visitors approach to greet the Prophet ﷺ, Abu Bakr and ’Umar.',tip:'Walk it slowly with salawat on your lips; no crowding, no raised voices.',stop:'G4'},
{id:'baqi',ar:'مقبرة البقيع',tags:['👤 Men enter','🕐 After Fajr & Asr','🌸 Sisters: salam from outside'],ll:[24.4668,39.6169],city:'madinah',g:'mharam',i:'🌿',n:'Jannat al-Baqi’',q:'Al Baqi Cemetery Madinah',d:'Resting place of ’Uthman (RA), most of the Prophet’s ﷺ wives, his daughters, his son Ibrahim, and — by common estimate — some ten thousand companions.',tip:'Open after Fajr and Asr; entry for men. Give the sunnah salam to the people of the graves.',stop:'G2'},
{id:'manakha',ar:'ساحة المناخة',ll:[24.4672,39.608],city:'madinah',g:'mharam',i:'🏙️',n:'Al Manakha Square',q:'Al Manakha Square Madinah',d:'The old caravan-halt and market ground west of the mosque — by tradition the site of the market the Prophet ﷺ chose for Madinah (the report that he said "this is your market", Ibn Majah 2233, is graded weak). Masjid al-Ghamamah stands on its edge.',tip:'Dates, cafés and evening buzz; a good meeting point for families.',stop:'G3'},
{id:'quranmuseum',ar:'معرض القرآن الكريم ومتحف السيرة النبوية',ll:[24.4683,39.6075],city:'madinah',g:'mharam',i:'📖',n:'Exhibition of the Holy Quran & the Prophet’s Biography',q:'International Fair and Museum of the Prophet Biography Madinah',d:'Two museums beside the Haram: rare mushafs and the science of the Quran, and a sensory walk through the seerah.',tip:'Wonderful with children; tours in many languages.'},
/* ── MADINAH · Historic mosques ── */
{id:'quba',ar:'مسجد قباء',ll:[24.4396,39.6172],city:'madinah',g:'mosques',i:'🕌',n:'Masjid Quba',q:'Quba Mosque Madinah',d:'The first mosque of Islam, "founded on taqwa" (Quran 9:108). Praying here with wudu from home earns the reward of an Umrah (Ibn Majah 1412).',tip:'The Prophet ﷺ visited every Saturday, walking or riding (Bukhari 1193) — make wudu at your hotel and go at least once.',stop:'R10'},
{id:'qiblatain',ar:'مسجد القبلتين',ll:[24.4841,39.5789],city:'madinah',g:'mosques',i:'🕌',n:'Masjid al-Qiblatayn',q:'Masjid al-Qiblatayn Madinah',d:'Where the revelation to turn the qibla from Jerusalem to Makkah came mid-prayer (Quran 2:144) — the congregation turned as one.',tip:'A short ride north-west of the Haram; combine with the Seven Mosques.',stop:'R8'},
{id:'jumuah',ar:'مسجد الجمعة',ll:[24.4455,39.6153],city:'madinah',g:'mosques',i:'🕌',n:'Masjid al-Jumu’ah',q:'Masjid Al Jumuah Madinah',d:'In the valley of Ranuna, where the Prophet ﷺ led his first Friday prayer, on the way from Quba into Madinah after the Hijrah.',tip:'Under 1 km north of Masjid Quba, near Quba Avenue — stop here if you walk to Quba.'},
{id:'ijabah',ar:'مسجد الإجابة',ll:[24.4737,39.618],city:'madinah',g:'mosques',i:'🤲',n:'Masjid al-Ijabah',q:'Masjid Al Ijabah Madinah',d:'The mosque of Banu Mu’awiyah where the Prophet ﷺ prayed and made a long dua: "I asked my Lord three things — He granted me two and withheld one" (Muslim 2890).',tip:'5 min from Baqi on Sitteen Street; pray two rakahs and make your own long dua.'},
{id:'ghamamah',ar:'مسجد الغمامة',ll:[24.4658,39.607],city:'madinah',g:'mosques',i:'🕌',n:'Masjid al-Ghamamah',q:'Masjid al-Ghamamah Madinah',d:'Where the Prophet ﷺ prayed Eid and istisqa (the rain prayer) — a cloud (ghamamah) is said to have shaded him here.',tip:'A 5-minute walk south-west of Masjid an-Nabawi, beside Al Manakha.'},
{id:'miqat',ar:'مسجد الميقات – أبيار علي',ll:[24.4137,39.5433],city:'madinah',g:'mosques',i:'🤍',n:'Masjid al-Miqat (Dhul-Hulayfah / Abyar Ali)',q:'Masjid Al Miqat Abyar Ali Madinah',d:'The miqat the Prophet ﷺ appointed for the people of Madinah (Bukhari 1524) — where you will assume ihram before travelling to Makkah, as he did.',tip:'9 km south-west. Showers, wudu areas and ihram shops on site; most buses stop here on request. The Haramain train does not stop here — put on ihram before boarding and make the intention before the train passes the miqat.'},
/* ── MADINAH · Battlefields, wells & history ── */
{id:'uhud',ar:'جبل أحد – مقبرة شهداء أحد',tags:['🕐 Best after Fajr'],ll:[24.504,39.613],city:'madinah',g:'history',i:'⛰️',n:'Mount Uhud & the Martyrs’ Cemetery',q:'Mount Uhud Madinah',d:'Site of the Battle of Uhud (3 AH) — resting place of Hamza (RA) and seventy martyrs. "Uhud is a mountain that loves us and we love it" (Bukhari 4083).',tip:'Visit after Fajr before the heat; give salam at the martyrs’ fence and climb the Archers’ Hill to understand the battle.',stop:'R5'},
{id:'khandaq',ar:'المساجد السبعة – الخندق',ll:[24.4768,39.596],city:'madinah',g:'history',i:'🕌',n:'The Trench Battlefield & the Seven Mosques',q:'Seven Mosques Madinah',d:'Where the Muslims dug the trench at the foot of Mount Sila’ in 5 AH and Allah sent the wind against the confederates (Quran 33:9). By tradition, small mosques mark the posts of the Prophet ﷺ, Salman, Abu Bakr, ’Umar, ’Ali and Fatimah.',tip:'Masjid al-Fath on the hill is where the Prophet ﷺ prayed for victory. Minutes from Qiblatayn.',stop:'R9'},
{id:'aqiq',ar:'وادي العقيق',ll:[24.43,39.56],city:'madinah',g:'history',i:'🏞️',n:'Wadi al-Aqiq',q:'Wadi Al Aqiq Madinah',d:'"A messenger came to me from my Lord and said: pray in this blessed valley" (Bukhari 1534). The valley of Madinah’s old estates and the path to the miqat.',tip:'You cross it on the way to Masjid al-Miqat — look out for it.'},
{id:'biruthman',ar:'بئر عثمان (بئر رومة)',ll:[24.4968,39.5822],city:'madinah',g:'history',i:'💧',n:'Bir Uthman (the Well of Rumah)',q:'Bir Uthman Madinah',d:'’Uthman (RA) bought this well and made it free for all Muslims after the Prophet ﷺ asked: "Who will buy the well of Rumah and put his bucket with the buckets of the Muslims, for better than it in Paradise?" (Tirmidhi 3703; see also Bukhari 2778). Its endowment still funds charity 1,400 years later.',tip:'A working date farm north of the city; a lesson in sadaqah jariyah.'},
{id:'salman',ar:'حديقة سلمان الفارسي',ll:[24.4442,39.6316],city:'madinah',g:'history',i:'🌴',n:'Garden of Salman al-Farisi',q:'Salman Al Farsi Garden Madinah',d:'The palm garden associated with Salman’s (RA) freedom — reports say the Prophet ﷺ planted the date palms with his own hands to pay the price of his manumission.',tip:'Near Quba; a quiet place to reflect on a seeker who crossed empires to find the truth.'},
{id:'hijazrail',ar:'محطة سكة حديد الحجاز',ll:[24.4617,39.6002],city:'madinah',g:'history',i:'🚂',n:'Al Hijaz Railway Station & Museum',q:'Hejaz Railway Museum Madinah',d:'The 1908 Ottoman terminus of the Damascus–Madinah line, built to carry pilgrims to the Haramain. The restored station is now a museum of Madinah’s history and the railway.',tip:'The museum is closed for restoration — check before you go. Hop-on hop-off stop.',stop:'R11'},
{id:'darmadinah',ar:'متحف دار المدينة',ll:[24.4767,39.6877],city:'madinah',g:'history',i:'🏛️',n:'Dar Al Madinah Museum',q:'Dar Al Madinah Museum',d:'The seerah and the history of Madinah with detailed models of the city across the ages.',tip:'Excellent with kids; guided tours available in English.'},
/* ── MADINAH · Hop-on hop-off & practical ── */
{id:'qubasq',ar:'شارع قباء',ll:[24.445,39.616],city:'madinah',g:'tour',i:'🚶',n:'Quba Square & Quba Avenue',q:'Quba Avenue Madinah',d:'The 3 km palm-lined pedestrian avenue from the Haram to Masjid Quba — walk it as the Prophet ﷺ did on his Saturday visits (Bukhari 1193).',tip:'Walk it at sunset; the bus brings you back. Hop-on hop-off stop.',stop:'R12'},
{id:'noormall',ar:'النور مول',ll:[24.496,39.5954],city:'madinah',g:'tour',i:'🛍️',n:'Al-Noor Mall',q:'Al Noor Mall Madinah',d:'One of Madinah’s largest malls — a practical hop-off for food courts, kids’ areas, pharmacies and gifts.',tip:'Handy between Uhud and Qiblatayn on the Red route.',stop:'R6'},
{id:'sultanah',ar:'شارع سلطانة (طريق أبي بكر الصديق)',ll:[24.4803,39.5966],city:'madinah',g:'tour',i:'🍽️',n:'Sultanah — Abu Bakr as-Siddiq Road',q:'Sultanah Road Madinah',d:'The main restaurant and shopping strip — Madinah dates, perfume, and the city’s best-known eateries.',tip:'Try the local Ajwa dates and the famous Madinah bukhari rice.',stop:'R7'}];

var QUIZ_LEVELS=[
{key:'u-found',short:'Foundations',j:'umrah',icon:'🕋',name:'Foundations of Umrah',desc:'What Umrah is and how it flows',study:{sub:'learn',id:'knowCard'},qs:'undefined',qs:[
{q:'Umrah is often called…',o:['The greater pilgrimage','The minor pilgrimage','The night journey','The farewell pilgrimage'],a:1,e:'Umrah is the "minor pilgrimage" — Hajj is the major one with fixed dates and extra rites.'},
{q:'True or false: Umrah can be performed at any time of the year.',tf:1,a:0,e:'True — unlike Hajj, Umrah has no fixed date. (Ramadan carries a special reward.) Hanafis dislike it on 9–13 Dhul Hijjah, and Saudi authorities pause Umrah permits around the Hajj season — check Nusuk for your dates.'},
{q:'The correct order of the Umrah rites is…',o:['Tawaf → Ihram → Sa’i → Halq','Ihram → Sa’i → Tawaf → Halq','Ihram → Tawaf → Sa’i → Halq/Taqsir','Sa’i → Tawaf → Ihram → Halq'],a:2,e:'Ihram at the miqat, then tawaf, then sa’i, then cutting the hair — in that order.'},
{q:'True or false: Umrah and Hajj are identical acts of worship.',tf:1,a:1,e:'False — Hajj adds Arafat, Muzdalifah, Mina and the fixed days of Dhul-Hijjah; Umrah is ihram, tawaf, sa’i and halq.'},
{q:'How many rounds (circuits) make one complete tawaf?',o:['3','5','7','10'],a:2,e:'Seven rounds, counter-clockwise, starting and ending at the Black Stone corner.'},
{q:'Sa’i begins at… and ends at…',o:['Marwah → Safa','Safa → Marwah','Either direction','The mataf'],a:1,e:'"Begin with what Allah began with" — Quran 2:158 mentions Safa first; 7 laps ending at Marwah.'},
{q:'When does the pilgrim stop reciting the talbiyah in Umrah?',o:['At the hotel','On seeing the Kaaba','When beginning tawaf','After sa’i'],a:2,e:'For the majority, the talbiyah continues from the miqat until you start your tawaf. Malikis stop it on entering the Haram (or, if ihram was from Tan’eem, on seeing the Kaaba) — if that is your school, your practice is sound.'},
{q:'What does "Labbayk" mean?',o:['Forgive me','Here I am (at Your service)','Allah is greatest','Praise be to Allah'],a:1,e:'Labbayk Allahumma labbayk — "Here I am, O Allah, here I am," answering the call to pilgrimage.'},
{q:'True or false: children may perform Umrah, with guardians handling the rites for the very young.',tf:1,a:0,e:'True — the Prophet ﷺ confirmed a child’s pilgrimage counts (Muslim 1336); parents make intention for infants.'},
{q:'True or false: an accepted pilgrimage returns you "as the day your mother bore you".',tf:1,a:0,e:'True — whoever performs it without obscenity or sin returns cleansed of sins (Bukhari 1521, Muslim 1350).'}
]},
{key:'u-ihram',short:'Ihram',j:'umrah',icon:'🤍',name:'Ihram & the Miqats',desc:'The sacred state and its boundaries',study:{sub:'learn',id:'knowCard'},qs:'undefined',qs:[
{q:'The miqat for those travelling to Makkah from Madinah is…',o:['Yalamlam','Qarn al-Manazil','Al-Juhfah','Dhul-Hulayfah (Abyar Ali)'],a:3,e:'Dhul-Hulayfah, just outside Madinah — the farthest miqat from Makkah (~450 km).'},
{q:'Most flights arriving from the east (UAE, Pakistan, India…) cross which miqat?',o:['Qarn al-Manazil (As-Sayl)','Al-Juhfah','Dhat Irq','Yalamlam'],a:0,e:'Qarn al-Manazil serves Najd, Taif and most eastern arrivals — be in ihram before the pilot’s announcement.'},
{q:'Which of these is prohibited in ihram?',o:['Wearing a wristwatch','Showering with plain water','Applying perfume','Wearing sandals'],a:2,e:'Scented products are prohibited. Watches, glasses, belts and unscented showers are all fine.'},
{q:'True or false: men may wear a money belt or waist pouch in ihram.',tf:1,a:0,e:'True — a belt is not fitted "stitched clothing"; it is how you keep your passport and cash safe.'},
{q:'True or false: a woman must wear a niqab (face veil) in ihram.',tf:1,a:1,e:'False — the opposite: the niqab is specifically not worn in ihram, and in the majority view gloves are not either (Bukhari 1838). Ordinary modest dress applies.'},
{q:'The two garments of a man’s ihram are called…',o:['Thobe & ghutrah','Izar & rida','Bisht & shemagh','Qamis & sirwal'],a:1,e:'The izar wraps the lower body, the rida drapes the upper body — both unstitched.'},
{q:'True or false: the intention for Umrah must be made before crossing the miqat.',tf:1,a:0,e:'True — crossing your miqat without ihram when intending Umrah requires returning to it, or else, for most scholars, a dam (a sheep slaughtered in the Haram).'},
{q:'True or false: a pilgrim flying to Jeddah may delay ihram until after landing.',tf:1,a:1,e:'False — Jeddah lies inside the miqat boundaries for most routes, so ihram must be assumed before the plane crosses the miqat.'},
{q:'Which is NOT an ihram violation?',o:['Applying perfume','Trimming nails','Wearing prescription glasses','Proposing marriage'],a:2,e:'Glasses, watches, rings, umbrellas and sandals are all fine in ihram.'},
{q:'True or false: if you break an ihram rule by genuine mistake or forgetfulness, your Umrah is ruined.',tf:1,a:1,e:'False — "Our Lord, do not hold us to account for what we forget or err in" (Quran 2:286). There is no sin — learn, correct, continue. But most schools still require a fidyah for some slips (e.g. shaving or clipping nails), and most hold intercourse invalidates it even if forgotten — ask a scholar.'}
]},
{key:'u-rites',short:'Tawaf',icon:'🔄',name:'Tawaf, Sa’i & Halq',desc:'The rites in fine detail',jx:{hajj:{desc:'Every tawaf & sa’i of your Hajj — and the Tamattu’ Umrah'},qiran:{desc:'Every tawaf & sa’i of your Hajj'},ifrad:{desc:'Every tawaf & sa’i of your Hajj'}},study:{sub:'learn',id:'knowCard'},rites:true,qs:'undefined',qs:[
{q:'Where does each round of tawaf begin?',o:['Maqam Ibrahim','The Black Stone (Hajar al-Aswad) corner','The Yamani corner','The Kaaba door'],a:1,e:'Face the Black Stone, say "Bismillahi wallahu akbar", then keep the Kaaba on your left.'},
{q:'What is raml?',o:['Brisk walking in the first 3 rounds of tawaf (men)','Jogging the whole sa’i','A type of dua','The stoning ritual'],a:0,e:'Men walk briskly with short steps in rounds 1–3 of the tawaf of arrival — the Umrah tawaf, or tawaf al-qudum for Qiran and Ifrad. Hanafis and Shafi’is also do it in a later tawaf that is followed by sa’i (such as a Tamattu’ pilgrim’s tawaf al-ifadah); Hanbalis do not, and Malikis do so only for a pilgrim who did no arrival tawaf. Women walk normally.'},
{q:'True or false: wudu is required for tawaf.',tf:1,a:0,e:'True — tawaf is like prayer (Tirmidhi 960); keep your wudu, and renew it if broken.'},
{q:'True or false: each round of tawaf has its own fixed, prescribed dua.',tf:1,a:1,e:'False — no dua is prescribed per round. Only "Rabbana atina…" between the Yamani corner and Black Stone is sunnah.'},
{q:'You cannot reach the Black Stone. What do you do?',o:['Push through the crowd','Skip that round','Point towards it and say Allahu Akbar','Wait until it clears'],a:2,e:'Gesture from afar with "Allahu Akbar" and continue — harming others for a sunnah is never right.'},
{q:'What dua is sunnah between the Yamani corner and the Black Stone?',o:['The talbiyah','Rabbana atina fid-dunya hasanah…','Ayat al-Kursi','Surah al-Fatihah'],a:1,e:'"Our Lord, give us good in this world and good in the Hereafter, and protect us from the punishment of the Fire." (Quran 2:201)'},
{q:'What is prayed immediately after tawaf?',o:['4 rakahs of Duha','2 rakahs behind Maqam Ibrahim','Salat al-Janazah','Nothing'],a:1,e:'Two rakahs behind Maqam Ibrahim (anywhere if crowded) — al-Kafirun then al-Ikhlas are sunnah.'},
{q:'True or false: Safa → Marwah counts as one full lap of sa’i.',tf:1,a:0,e:'True — one direction is one lap; seven laps finish on Marwah.'},
{q:'True or false: women jog between the green lights during sa’i.',tf:1,a:1,e:'False — the light jog there is for men; women walk throughout.'},
{q:'How does a woman come out of ihram — after sa’i in Umrah, or after the stoning on the 10th in Hajj?',o:['Shave her head','Trim a fingertip’s length of hair','Cut a single strand','Nothing required'],a:1,e:'Women trim about a fingertip’s length (~2 cm) from the ends — never shave: “Shaving is not a duty laid on women; only clipping the hair is incumbent on them.”',src:'Abu Dawud 1984'},
{q:'True or false: for men, shaving the head (halq) carries more reward than trimming (taqsir).',tf:1,a:0,e:'True — the Prophet ﷺ made dua for those who shave three times, and once for those who trim (Bukhari 1728, Muslim 1302).'},
{j:'hajj',ht:['tamattu'],p1:1,q:'Tamattu’: to end your Part 1 Umrah, the Prophet ﷺ told those without a hady to…',o:['Shave completely and stay in ihram until the 8th','Cut their hair short and leave ihram','Leave the hair untouched and leave ihram anyway','Cut just three hairs and stay in ihram'],a:1,e:'“…perform tawaf of the Ka’bah and between Safa and Marwah, then cut short his hair and finish his ihram, and later assume ihram for Hajj.” Many scholars advise a trim when the 8th is near, so there is hair to shave on the Day of Nahr.',src:'Bukhari 1691 · Muslim 1218'}
]},
{key:'u-hist',short:'History',icon:'🕰️',name:'History of Makkah & the Kaaba',desc:'The story behind every rite',study:{sub:'learn',id:'histCard'},qs:'undefined',qs:[
{q:'Who raised the foundations of the Kaaba?',o:['Adam alone','Ibrahim & Isma’il','Quraysh','Musa & Harun'],a:1,e:'"And when Ibrahim was raising the foundations of the House with Isma’il…" (Quran 2:127).'},
{q:'The Kaaba is described in the Quran as…',o:['The first House established for mankind','The largest building of its time','A gift to Quraysh','The house of Adam'],a:0,e:'"The first House established for mankind was that at Bakkah — blessed and a guidance for the worlds." (Quran 3:96)'},
{q:'The well of Zamzam sprang for…',o:['Ibrahim on his journey','Hajar and baby Isma’il','The army of the Elephant','Quraysh pilgrims'],a:1,e:'After Hajar’s seventh run between Safa and Marwah, an angel struck the ground with his heel (or wing) at the place of Zamzam, near where baby Isma’il lay, and the water gushed out (Bukhari 3364).'},
{q:'Sa’i between Safa and Marwah commemorates…',o:['The Hijrah route','Hajar’s search for water','The conquest of Makkah','The Farewell Sermon'],a:1,e:'Your seven laps re-enact Hajar’s seven runs of pure trust in Allah — "Then He will not abandon us." (Bukhari 3364)'},
{q:'True or false: the Black Stone descended from Paradise whiter than milk.',tf:1,a:0,e:'True — "…and the sins of the children of Adam blackened it." (Tirmidhi 877)'},
{q:'How did the young Muhammad ﷺ resolve the Quraysh dispute over placing the Black Stone?',o:['He placed it himself secretly','He drew lots','He laid it on a cloth for all tribes to lift together','He told them to leave it out'],a:2,e:'Every tribe lifted a corner of the cloth, and al-Amin ﷺ set the Stone in place with his own hands.'},
{q:'The first revelation came to the Prophet ﷺ in…',o:['The cave of Thawr','Masjid al-Haram','The cave of Hira on Jabal an-Nour','Mina'],a:2,e:'"Iqra — Read!" (Quran 96:1) descended in Hira, the cave above Makkah (Bukhari 3).'},
{q:'True or false: Muslims always prayed towards the Kaaba from the beginning of Islam.',tf:1,a:1,e:'False — they first faced Jerusalem; the qibla changed mid-prayer (~2 AH) — Quran 2:144, commemorated at Masjid al-Qiblatayn.'},
{q:'Maqam Ibrahim is…',o:['Ibrahim’s grave','The stone he stood on while building the Kaaba','The door of the Kaaba','A minaret'],a:1,e:'The standing-stone of Ibrahim (AS) — "Take the Maqam of Ibrahim as a place of prayer." (Quran 2:125)'},
{q:'During the Hijrah, the Prophet ﷺ and Abu Bakr hid in…',o:['The cave of Hira','The cave of Thawr','Masjid Quba','Jabal Uhud'],a:1,e:'Three nights in Thawr — "Do not grieve; indeed Allah is with us." (Quran 9:40)'},
{q:'True or false: praying inside Hijr Isma’il counts as praying inside the Kaaba.',tf:1,a:0,e:'True — "it is a part of the House" (Tirmidhi 876): the part next to the Kaaba wall, roughly six cubits deep, lay inside the original Kaaba (Muslim 1333), so pray close to the wall. A rare chance most pilgrims miss!'}
]},
{key:'u-virt',short:'Virtues',icon:'🌟',name:'Virtues of Umrah & the Haram',desc:'The rewards that await you',jx:{hajj:{name:'Virtues of Hajj & the Haram',desc:'The rewards of an accepted Hajj'}},study:{sub:'learn',id:'virtCard'},qs:'undefined',qs:[
{j:'umrah',q:'"Umrah to Umrah is an expiation for…"',o:['All future sins','The sins committed between them','Major sins only','Debts'],a:1,e:'Bukhari 1773 — and the accepted Hajj has no reward but Paradise.'},
{j:'umrah',q:'What does "an Umrah in Ramadan" equal, according to the Prophet ﷺ?',o:['10 Umrahs','A Hajj with him ﷺ','Laylatul Qadr','A year of fasting'],a:1,e:'"An Umrah in Ramadan is equal to a Hajj" (Bukhari 1782) — and in one narration: "a Hajj with me" (Bukhari 1863, Muslim 1256).'},
{q:'One prayer in Masjid al-Haram equals how many elsewhere?',o:['1,000','10,000','50,000','100,000'],a:3,e:'100,000 prayers (Ibn Majah 1406) — one Fajr there outweighs decades of prayer elsewhere.'},
{q:'True or false: "Follow up between Hajj and Umrah, for they remove poverty and sins…"',tf:1,a:0,e:'True — "…as the bellows removes the dross of iron, gold and silver." (Tirmidhi 810)'},
{q:'Pilgrims are described by the Prophet ﷺ as…',o:['The soldiers of Allah','The delegation (guests) of Allah','The neighbours of the Kaaba','The people of the cloak'],a:1,e:'"The delegation of Allah are three: the one who fights in His cause, the one performing Hajj and the one performing Umrah." (Nasai 2625)'},
{q:'True or false: the Prophet ﷺ called Hajj and Umrah the jihad of the elderly, the young, the weak and women.',tf:1,a:0,e:'True — "The jihad of the elderly, the young, the weak and women is Hajj and Umrah" (Nasai 2626); and to Aishah (RA): "jihad in which there is no fighting" (Ibn Majah 2901).'},
{q:'Completing tawaf while keeping count of it is likened to…',o:['Fasting a month','Freeing a slave','A year of charity','Building a mosque'],a:1,e:'Tirmidhi 959 — and with every step a sin is erased and a good deed recorded.'},
{q:'"Zamzam water is…"',o:['Only for the sick','For whatever it is drunk for','Only for pilgrims','For wudu only'],a:1,e:'Ibn Majah 3062 — drink it with a specific dua: knowledge, cure, forgiveness, whatever you need.'},
{q:'True or false: the Prophet ﷺ called Makkah the most beloved of Allah’s land to Allah.',tf:1,a:0,e:'True — "Had I not been driven out of you, I would never have left." (Tirmidhi 3925)'},
{q:'True or false: because the Haram multiplies reward, wrongdoing there is also graver.',tf:1,a:0,e:'True — Allah warns against "deviation to wrongdoing" in the Haram (Quran 22:25). Guard your tongue and gaze doubly.'},
{j:'hajj',q:'“Umrah to Umrah is an expiation for what is between them, and the reward of an accepted Hajj (mabrur) is…”',o:['Nothing but Paradise','A long and healthy life','Wealth and many children','A good name among people'],a:0,e:'“…nothing except Paradise.” Mabrur: done sincerely, the Sunnah way, without sin — and a life that changes after it.',src:'Bukhari 1773 · Muslim 1349'},
{j:'hajj',q:'“Whoever performs Hajj for Allah, with no intimacy and no sin, returns…”',o:['With double the reward of Umrah','As on the day his mother bore him','With a title of honour','With a share of the sacrifice'],a:1,e:'“…as the day his mother bore him” — free of sins, as if born anew.',src:'Bukhari 1521 · Muslim 1350'},
{j:'hajj',q:'“Do you not know that Islam wipes out what came before it, the Hijrah wipes out what came before it, and ___ wipes out what came before it?”',o:['Fasting','Hajj','Charity','Jumu’ah'],a:1,e:'Said to Amr ibn al-As (RA) when he accepted Islam and asked to be forgiven: “…and Hajj wipes out what came before it.”',src:'Muslim 121'},
{j:'hajj',q:'“There are no days in which righteous deeds are more beloved to Allah than…”',o:['These ten days — the first ten of Dhul Hijjah','The first ten days of Muharram','The middle of Sha’ban','Any Monday and Thursday'],a:0,e:'“…than these ten days.” They asked: “Not even jihad in the path of Allah?” He said: “Not even jihad, except a man who goes out with himself and his wealth and returns with nothing.” Your Hajj sits inside the best days of the year.',src:'Tirmidhi 757 · Abu Dawud 2438 · Bukhari 969'}
]},
{key:'u-mad',short:'Madinah',icon:'🕌',name:'Madinah — City of the Prophet ﷺ',desc:'Virtues & etiquette of the visit',study:{sub:'learn',id:'madCard'},qs:'undefined',qs:[
{q:'One prayer in Masjid an-Nabawi is better than…',o:['100 elsewhere','1,000 elsewhere (except al-Masjid al-Haram)','100,000 elsewhere','70 elsewhere'],a:1,e:'Bukhari 1190 — a thousand-fold, exceeded only by Masjid al-Haram.'},
{q:'The Rawdah is described by the Prophet ﷺ as…',o:['The best of gardens','A garden from the gardens of Paradise','His private courtyard','The first masjid'],a:1,e:'"Between my house and my minbar is a garden from the gardens of Paradise." (Bukhari 1195)'},
{q:'Visiting the Rawdah requires…',o:['Nothing — walk in anytime','A paid ticket','A free timed permit via the Nusuk app','A tour guide'],a:2,e:'Rawdah slots open only a short time ahead — check Nusuk as your dates approach and book as soon as they open.'},
{q:'Praying in Masjid Quba (with wudu from home) equals…',o:['A Hajj','An Umrah','40 years of prayer','Nothing specific'],a:1,e:'Ibn Majah 1412 — and the Prophet ﷺ visited Quba every Saturday.'},
{q:'True or false: the Prophet ﷺ declared Madinah a sacred sanctuary (haram).',tf:1,a:0,e:'True — he declared sacred what lies between its two lava plains, as Ibrahim declared Makkah sacred (Muslim 1374). Most scholars forbid hunting and cutting its trees, though its rules are lighter than Makkah’s; Hanafis do not give it Makkah’s rulings.'},
{q:'True or false: the Dajjal and the plague can enter Madinah.',tf:1,a:1,e:'False — "There are angels at the entrances of Madinah: neither plague nor the Dajjal can enter it." (Bukhari 1880)'},
{q:'Masjid al-Qiblatayn is famous because…',o:['It has two minarets','The qibla changed there mid-prayer','It is the oldest mosque','The Prophet ﷺ was born there'],a:1,e:'The congregation turned from Jerusalem to the Kaaba in a single prayer (Quran 2:144).'},
{q:'The Prophet ﷺ said about Uhud…',o:['"It is a cursed mountain"','"It is a mountain that loves us and we love it"','"Never climb it"','"It guards the city"'],a:1,e:'Bukhari 4083 — at its foot rest Hamza (RA) and the seventy martyrs of Uhud.'},
{q:'Who rests beside the Prophet ﷺ in his chamber?',o:['Hamza & Abbas','Abu Bakr & Umar','Uthman & Ali','His wives'],a:1,e:'His two closest companions and successors — greet all three with a calm salam.'},
{q:'True or false: visiting Madinah is a required rite of Umrah or Hajj.',tf:1,a:1,e:'False — beloved and highly virtuous (one of the three mosques worth travelling to — Bukhari 1189), but not part of the rites of Umrah or Hajj, and it needs no ihram.'},
{q:'"Whoever eats seven ___ dates in the morning will not be harmed that day by poison or magic."',o:['Sukkari','Ajwa','Medjool','Barhi'],a:1,e:'Ajwa dates of Madinah (Bukhari 5779) — the perfect gift to bring home.'},
{q:'True or false: the Prophet ﷺ will intercede for the one who dies in Madinah.',tf:1,a:0,e:'True — "Whoever is able to die in Madinah, let him die there, for I will intercede for him." (Tirmidhi 3917)'}
]},
{key:'u-fiqh',short:'Fiqh',j:'umrah',icon:'⚖️',name:'Fiqh essentials — Q&A',desc:'Rulings, nuances & common doubts',study:{sub:'learn',id:'qaCard'},qs:'undefined',qs:[
{q:'Can you perform Umrah on behalf of a deceased parent?',o:['Never','Yes — after having performed your own Umrah first','Only during Hajj season','Only for mothers'],a:1,e:'Badal is established: "Labbayk on behalf of Shubrumah…" — the Prophet ﷺ said to perform your own (Hajj) first, then his (Abu Dawud 1811). Shafi’is and Hanbalis make this a condition; Hanafis and Malikis hold a proxy pilgrimage before your own is valid but disliked — doing your own first is the safe course.'},
{q:'True or false: you may perform a second Umrah in the same trip by taking ihram from Tan’eem.',tf:1,a:0,e:'True — after her Hajj, the Prophet ﷺ sent Aishah (RA) with her brother to Tan’eem for an Umrah (Bukhari 1784, 1785). Most scholars allow it; Malikis dislike more than one Umrah a year, and some (e.g. Ibn Taymiyyah) prefer extra tawaf instead.'},
{q:'How many Umrahs did the Prophet ﷺ perform?',o:['One','Two','Four','Seven'],a:2,e:'Four: Hudaybiyyah, al-Qada, al-Ji’ranah, and the one with his Hajj (Bukhari 1778, 1780).'},
{q:'Your wudu breaks in round 5 of tawaf. What happens?',o:['The whole Umrah is invalid','Renew wudu and return to the tawaf','You must restart from round 1 tomorrow','Nothing — wudu is optional'],a:1,e:'Renew your wudu and return. Hanafis and Shafi’is build on the completed rounds (resume the interrupted round, or repeat it to be safe); Malikis and Hanbalis restart the tawaf from round 1. Either way, no panic — nothing is ruined.'},
{q:'True or false: you may pause tawaf or sa’i to rest, drink Zamzam, or join the obligatory prayer, then continue.',tf:1,a:0,e:'True — continue from where you left off. "Allah intends for you ease" (Quran 2:185).'},
{q:'A woman’s period begins after ihram. She…',o:['Cannot do Umrah at all','Continues everything except tawaf (and the sa’i after it) until pure','Does tawaf anyway','Must leave Makkah immediately'],a:1,e:'Ihram, talbiyah, dhikr and dua all continue; only the tawaf (and its sa’i) wait for purity (Bukhari 305).'},
{q:'True or false: skipping raml or idtiba’ invalidates the tawaf.',tf:1,a:1,e:'False — both are sunnah for men in the arrival tawaf (Hanafis and Shafi’is: also in a later tawaf followed by sa’i); leaving them affects nothing.'},
{q:'Tawaf in a wheelchair or on the upper floors is…',o:['Invalid','Valid only for the elderly','Fully valid','Half reward'],a:2,e:'Fully valid — the Prophet ﷺ himself performed tawaf mounted (Bukhari 1607).'},
{q:'True or false: speaking during tawaf breaks it, exactly like speaking in salah.',tf:1,a:1,e:'False — "Tawaf around the House is like salah, except that you speak in it; so whoever speaks, let him speak only good." (Tirmidhi 960)'},
{q:'True or false: a man’s ihram garments must be brand new and pure white.',tf:1,a:1,e:'False — the requirement is an izar and rida not tailored to the body (a hem or seam is fine); clean is expected, white is recommended, and new is not required. Spares and washing are fine.'}
]},
{key:'h-days',short:'Days of Hajj',j:'off',icon:'⛺',name:'The days of Hajj',desc:'Arafah, Muzdalifah, Mina and the stoning',study:{sub:'learn',id:'knowCard'},qs:'undefined',qs:[
{q:'"Hajj is ___," said the Prophet ﷺ.',o:['Mina','Arafah','Tawaf','Muzdalifah'],a:1,e:'"Hajj is Arafah" (Tirmidhi 889) — standing there on the 9th is the pillar without which there is no Hajj.'},
{q:'True or false: the standing at Arafah is valid only on Jabal ar-Rahmah.',tf:1,a:1,e:'False — "all of Arafat is a place of standing" (Ibn Majah 3012); only the hollow of Uranah lies outside it. Sitting in the tent counts in full.'},
{q:'At Arafah, Dhuhr and Asr are prayed…',o:['Each at its own time, in full','Together at Dhuhr time, two rakahs each','Together at Asr time','Not at all'],a:1,e:'One adhan and two iqamahs (Muslim 1218), at Dhuhr time (Bukhari 1662), shortened to two rakahs each — then the standing begins. That is the Sunnah and majority practice; Abu Hanifa allowed combining only behind the imam, so many Hanafi groups pray each at its own time in the tents, and schools differ on who shortens — follow your group’s scholar.'},
{q:'On the 10th of Dhul Hijjah, which jamarah is stoned?',o:['All three','The small one only','Jamrat al-Aqabah only','The middle one only'],a:2,e:'Only the big pillar (Aqabah) on the 10th — seven pebbles, takbir with each (Bukhari 1750). All three are stoned on the 11th–13th.'},
{q:'How many pebbles are thrown at each jamarah?',o:['3','5','7','10'],a:2,e:'Seven, one at a time, chickpea-sized — "beware of going to extremes" (Ibn Majah 3029).'},
{q:'True or false: the weak, the elderly and women may leave Muzdalifah after midnight.',tf:1,a:0,e:'True — the Prophet ﷺ sent the weak of his family ahead on the night of Muzdalifah (Bukhari 1678); Asma (RA) left when the moon had set (Bukhari 1679).'},
{q:'Who owes a hady (sacrifice)?',o:['Every pilgrim','Tamattu’ and Qiran pilgrims','Ifrad pilgrims only','Nobody — it is optional'],a:1,e:'"Whoever joins Umrah to Hajj must offer what hady is easy" (Quran 2:196) — Tamattu’ and Qiran. Ifrad owes none.'},
{q:'When does the Hajj pilgrim stop the talbiyah?',o:['On reaching Mina','At sunset on Arafah','At the first pebble of Jamrat al-Aqabah','After tawaf al-ifadah'],a:2,e:'He ﷺ "kept reciting the talbiyah till he did the rami of Jamrat al-Aqabah" (Bukhari 1685). Some continue to the last pebble. Malikis stop it on arriving at Arafah after midday on the 9th — if that is your school, your practice is sound.'},
{q:'On the 11th and 12th, after which jamarat do you stop for a long dua?',o:['After all three','After the first and the second only','After Aqabah only','There is no dua'],a:1,e:'Takbir with each pebble, then a long dua facing the qibla after the small and the middle — and none after Aqabah (Bukhari 1753).'},
{q:'True or false: a menstruating woman must perform tawaf al-wada before leaving.',tf:1,a:1,e:'False — "except the menstruating women, who were excused" (Bukhari 1755). Tawaf al-ifadah, however, must still be done once she is pure.'},
{q:'Stoning on the 11th, 12th and 13th is done…',o:['Before Fajr','Before midday','After the sun passes its zenith (zawal)','Only at night'],a:2,e:'"We used to wait till the sun declined, then do the rami" (Bukhari 1746; Tirmidhi 894). Those who cannot may stone at night.'},
{q:'True or false: whoever leaves Mina on the 12th must be out before sunset — or else stay and stone on the 13th.',tf:1,a:0,e:'True — "whoever hastens in two days there is no sin on him" (Quran 2:203); for the majority, sunset inside Mina means staying for the 13th.'},
{q:'Which of these is a pillar (rukn) of Hajj that no dam can replace?',o:['The night at Muzdalifah','Tawaf al-ifadah','The farewell tawaf','Stoning the jamarat'],a:1,e:'Tawaf al-ifadah is a pillar (Quran 22:29): the Hajj stays incomplete until it is done. For the majority, the other three are wajibs, repaired by a dam if missed (Malikis count the farewell tawaf as sunnah).'}
]},
{key:'h-found',short:'Foundations',j:'hajj',icon:'📜',mk:'scroll',name:'Foundations of Hajj',desc:'Pillars, wajibs, sunnahs & the three types',study:{sub:'learn',id:'knowCard'},qs:'undefined',qs:[
{q:'Hajj is obligatory on an able adult Muslim…',o:['Every year','Once in a lifetime','Every five years','Only for those who live near Makkah'],a:1,e:'Once in a lifetime. When a man asked “every year?”, the Prophet ﷺ said: “If I said yes, it would become obligatory — and you would not be able to do it.”',src:'Muslim 1337 · Quran 3:97'},
{q:'True or false: a child’s Hajj counts as his obligatory Hajj of Islam.',tf:1,a:1,e:'False — a child’s Hajj is valid: when a woman held up a child and asked “Is there Hajj for this one?”, the Prophet ﷺ said “Yes, and you will have a reward.” But the scholars agree he must perform it again after puberty to fulfil the obligation.',src:'Muslim 1336 · Tirmidhi 926'},
{q:'The rites of Hajj fall on which days of Dhul Hijjah?',o:['1st–5th','8th–13th','15th–20th','25th–30th'],a:1,e:'From the 8th (Tarwiyah) to the 12th — or the 13th if you stay in Mina. The 9th is Arafah and the 10th is Eid al-Adha.',src:'Muslim 1218 · Quran 2:203'},
{q:'Which two rites are pillars of Hajj in all four schools?',o:['The standing at Arafah and tawaf al-ifadah','The night at Muzdalifah and the stoning','The arrival tawaf and the farewell tawaf','The sacrifice and the shave'],a:0,e:'“Hajj is Arafah”, and tawaf al-ifadah is commanded in the Quran. Most schools also count ihram and sa’i as pillars (for Hanafis ihram is a condition and sa’i a wajib), and Shafi’is add the shave or trim.',src:'Tirmidhi 889 · Quran 22:29'},
{q:'A pilgrim leaves out a wajib of Hajj — say, the stoning — without an excuse. What follows?',o:['The Hajj is void and must be repeated next year','The Hajj stands, and a dam (a sheep slaughtered in the Haram) is owed','The Hajj stands, and nothing at all is owed','The Hajj stands once he fasts for three days'],a:1,e:'“Whoever forgets something of his rites or leaves it out, let him shed blood” (Ibn Abbas). A missed wajib is repaired with a dam; a missed pillar is not — it must be done. Which rites are wajib differs a little between the schools, so learn your group’s list.',src:'Ibn Abbas in al-Muwatta’ (Hajj; mawquf, sahih)'},
{q:'Which of these is a sunnah of Hajj — leaving it costs nothing?',o:['Standing at Arafah','Tawaf al-ifadah','Spending the 8th and the night before Arafah in Mina','Stoning the jamarat on the days of tashreeq'],a:2,e:'The Prophet ﷺ prayed Dhuhr to Fajr in Mina on the 8th, and the four schools agree this stay is a sunnah — nothing is owed for missing it. Arafah and tawaf al-ifadah are pillars; the stoning is a wajib.',src:'Muslim 1218'},
{q:'Ihram for Umrah at the miqat (in the months of Hajj), out of ihram after it, then a fresh ihram for Hajj on the 8th. Which type of Hajj is this?',o:['Ifrad','Qiran','Tamattu’','Badal'],a:2,e:'Tamattu’ — what the Prophet ﷺ told his companions without a sacrificial animal to do: tawaf, sa’i, cut the hair, leave ihram, then ihram for Hajj later. A hady is owed.',src:'Bukhari 1691 · Bukhari 1572'},
{q:'A Qiran pilgrim…',o:['Does Umrah, leaves ihram, then a fresh ihram for Hajj on the 8th','Joins Umrah and Hajj in one ihram and stays in it until the 10th','Enters ihram for Hajj alone, with no Umrah joined to it','Does the Hajj first, then an Umrah from Tan’eem after it'],a:1,e:'“Whoever has the hady with him should assume ihram for Hajj along with Umrah, and should not finish the ihram till he finishes both.” Qiran also owes a hady.',src:'Bukhari 1556 · Muslim 1211'},
{q:'Who owes a hady (sacrifice)?',o:['Every pilgrim, whatever the type of Hajj','Tamattu’ and Qiran pilgrims whose family is not present at al-Masjid al-Haram','Ifrad pilgrims only, since they did no Umrah','Nobody — the sacrifice on Eid is optional'],a:1,e:'“Whoever performs Umrah [during the Hajj months] followed by Hajj [offers] what can be obtained with ease of sacrificial animals… This is for those whose family is not in the area of al-Masjid al-Haram.” Qiran counts as joining the two, so it owes a hady too; Ifrad owes none. Who counts as a resident differs a little between the schools.',src:'Quran 2:196'},
{q:'True or false: Quran 2:197 rules out sexual relations, disobedience and disputing for the one who has entered Hajj.',tf:1,a:0,e:'True — “…there is no sexual relations and no disobedience and no disputing during Hajj.” The manners of the pilgrim start the moment you enter ihram.',src:'Quran 2:197'},
{q:'When did the Prophet ﷺ perform his one Hajj after the Hijrah?',o:['In 2 AH','In 8 AH, right after the Conquest','In 10 AH — the Farewell Hajj','He never performed Hajj'],a:2,e:'He stayed nine years in Madinah without performing Hajj, then announced it in the tenth year — his only Hajj after the Hijrah.',src:'Muslim 1218 · Bukhari 4404'},
{ht:['tamattu'],p1:1,q:'As a Tamattu’ pilgrim, your Part 1 Umrah runs in which order?',o:['Tawaf → ihram → sa’i → cut, then out of ihram until the 8th','Ihram → sa’i → tawaf → cut, then out of ihram until the 8th','Ihram → tawaf → sa’i → cut, then out of ihram until the 8th','Ihram → tawaf → sa’i, then in ihram until the 10th'],a:2,e:'Ihram at the miqat, tawaf, sa’i, then cut the hair and leave ihram. You enter ihram again for Hajj on the 8th.',src:'Bukhari 1691'},
{ht:['qiran','ifrad'],q:'On arrival in Makkah, a pilgrim who stays in Qiran or Ifrad…',o:['Does tawaf and sa’i, then cuts the hair and leaves ihram','Does the arrival tawaf (and may do the sa’i now) but no cut — in ihram until the 10th','Leaves ihram on arrival and enters it again on the 8th','Does a separate Umrah first, like a Tamattu’ pilgrim'],a:1,e:'“Whoever had assumed ihram for Hajj, or for Hajj and Umrah together, did not finish the ihram till the Day of Sacrifice.” No cutting until after the stoning on the 10th. (Hanbalis recommend that one who has no hady with him change his ihram to an Umrah on arrival — he then becomes a Tamattu’ pilgrim.)',src:'Bukhari 1562 · Bukhari 1615'}
]},
{key:'h-ihram',short:'Ihram & talbiyah',j:'hajj',icon:'🤍',mk:'heart',name:'Ihram for Hajj & the talbiyah',desc:'Miqats, the 8th, the talbiyah & the ihram rules',study:{sub:'learn',id:'knowCard'},qs:'undefined',qs:[
{q:'A pilgrim flying in for Hajj enters ihram…',o:['After landing, at Jeddah airport','Before the plane crosses the miqat on its route','At the hotel in Makkah, after resting','On the 8th of Dhul Hijjah, from Mina'],a:1,e:'The miqats are “for those who pass through them intending Hajj or Umrah” — on a plane, at the miqat announcement. Tamattu’: ihram for Umrah; Qiran: for Umrah and Hajj; Ifrad: for Hajj.',src:'Bukhari 1524'},
{q:'The miqat for pilgrims travelling to Makkah from Madinah is…',o:['Yalamlam (As-Sa’diyyah)','Qarn al-Manazil (As-Sayl)','Al-Juhfah (near Rabigh)','Dhul-Hulayfah (Abyar Ali)'],a:3,e:'Dhul-Hulayfah, just outside Madinah — the miqat the Prophet ﷺ set for the people of Madinah, and the one he used for his own Hajj.',src:'Bukhari 1524 · Muslim 1218'},
{ht:['tamattu'],q:'On the 8th, a Tamattu’ pilgrim enters ihram for Hajj from…',o:['The miqat they flew over on arrival','Masjid Aisha at Tan’eem, outside the Haram','Where they are staying in Makkah','The plain of Arafah itself'],a:2,e:'“…and the people of Makkah from Makkah.” The companions who had done Umrah entered ihram for Hajj as they set out for Mina — no trip to a miqat is needed.',src:'Bukhari 1526 · Muslim 1218'},
{ht:['qiran','ifrad'],q:'On the 8th, a Qiran or Ifrad pilgrim…',o:['Makes a fresh ihram and intention at the hotel','Is still in the ihram from the miqat, and sets out for Mina','Travels back to the miqat to renew the ihram','Leaves ihram for the day and re-enters it at Arafah'],a:1,e:'Your ihram has run since the miqat; nothing new is needed. You leave it only after the stoning and the cut on the 10th.',src:'Bukhari 1562'},
{q:'Complete the talbiyah: “…inna al-hamda wan-ni‘mata laka wal-___, la sharika lak.”',o:['mulk (the sovereignty)','‘izzah (the honour)','jannah (Paradise)','rahmah (the mercy)'],a:0,e:'“Labbayk Allahumma labbayk, labbayk la sharika laka labbayk, inna al-hamda wan-ni‘mata laka wal-mulk, la sharika lak” — “Here I am, O Allah, here I am…” — the Prophet’s ﷺ own wording.',src:'Bukhari 1549'},
{q:'How are men told to recite the talbiyah?',o:['Silently, in the heart only, so as not to show off','Aloud — Jibril ordered that the companions raise their voices','Once only, at the miqat, and then not again','Only in congregation, led by the group leader'],a:1,e:'“Jibril came to me and commanded me to order my companions to raise their voices with the talbiyah.” Sisters recite it in a low voice.',src:'Abu Dawud 1814 · Tirmidhi 829'},
{q:'For the majority, when does the Hajj pilgrim stop the talbiyah?',o:['On beginning tawaf, as in Umrah','At sunset on the day of Arafah','On stoning Jamrat al-Aqabah on the 10th','After tawaf al-ifadah'],a:2,e:'He ﷺ “kept reciting the talbiyah till he did the rami of Jamrat al-Aqabah.” Stopping at tawaf is the rule of Umrah, not Hajj. Some stop at the last pebble; Malikis stop on reaching Arafah after midday on the 9th — if that is your school, your practice is sound.',src:'Bukhari 1685 · Muslim 1282'},
{ht:['tamattu'],p1:1,q:'Tamattu’: when does the talbiyah of your Part 1 Umrah end, for the majority?',o:['It runs on until the stoning on the 10th','As the Umrah’s tawaf begins — a new talbiyah starts on the 8th','On first seeing the buildings of Makkah','After the last lap of the sa’i, at Marwah'],a:1,e:'Ibn Abbas (RA): the talbiyah of Umrah stops on touching the Black Stone. Your Hajj then has its own talbiyah, from the ihram on the 8th until Jamrat al-Aqabah. Malikis stop the Umrah talbiyah on entering the Haram.',src:'Tirmidhi 919 · Bukhari 1685'},
{q:'Which of these does the Quran itself forbid in ihram?',o:['Hunting land game','Fishing at sea','Sitting in the shade of a tent','Changing your ihram garments'],a:0,e:'“Do not kill game while you are in ihram” — while “game of the sea” stays lawful. The Prophet ﷺ himself had a tent pitched for him at Namirah on the day of Arafah.',src:'Quran 5:95–96 · Muslim 1218'},
{q:'In ihram, a man may NOT wear…',o:['A belt or a money pouch round the waist','Sandals that leave the ankles uncovered','A shirt, trousers, turban or hooded cloak','A wristwatch, glasses or a ring'],a:2,e:'Asked what the muhrim wears, the Prophet ﷺ said: “He should not wear a shirt, a turban, trousers, a hooded cloak or leather socks” — nor clothes scented with saffron or wars.',src:'Bukhari 1542'},
{q:'A woman in ihram should not…',o:['Wear her ordinary modest clothes','Tie a niqab over her face','Wear coloured clothes','Wear socks'],a:1,e:'“The woman in ihram should not wear a niqab or gloves.” The niqab is agreed on; most scholars also forbid gloves, while Hanafis permit them. Her ordinary modest clothing in any colour is her ihram.',src:'Bukhari 1838'},
{q:'True or false: in ihram you may wash with plain water and change into a clean set of ihram garments.',tf:1,a:0,e:'True — Abu Ayyub (RA) showed how the Prophet ﷺ washed his head while in ihram. Swapping a soiled set for a clean one is fine; only scented products are avoided.',src:'Bukhari 1840'},
{q:'Which words open a Qiran Hajj at the miqat?',o:['Labbayk Allahumma ‘Umrah','Labbayk ‘Umratan wa Hajjan','Labbayk Allahumma Hajjan','Labbayk ‘an Shubrumah'],a:1,e:'Anas (RA) heard the Prophet ﷺ say “Labbayka ‘umratan wa hajjan.” Tamattu’ says “‘Umrah”; Ifrad says “Hajjan”; “‘an …” is said only when performing for someone else.',src:'Muslim 1232'}
]},
{key:'h-arafah',short:'Mina & Arafah',j:'hajj',icon:'🏔',mk:'mount',name:'The 8th & 9th — Mina & Arafah',desc:'Tarwiyah, the standing & the dua of Arafah',study:{tab:'umrah',sub:'steps',id:'sec-hd9'},rites:true,qs:'undefined',qs:[
{q:'The 8th of Dhul Hijjah is called…',o:['Yawm an-Nahr','Yawm at-Tarwiyah','Yawm al-Qarr','Yawm ‘Arafah'],a:1,e:'Yawm at-Tarwiyah — the day the companions set out for Mina in ihram for Hajj. The 9th is Arafah, the 10th the Day of Nahr, and the 11th the Day of Qarr (staying).',src:'Muslim 1218 · Abu Dawud 1765'},
{q:'On the 8th in Mina, the Prophet ﷺ…',o:['Prayed Dhuhr, Asr, Maghrib, Isha and Fajr there, each in its own time','Combined Dhuhr with Asr and Maghrib with Isha, as at Arafah','Stood in dua on Jabal ar-Rahmah until sunset','Stoned the three jamarat after midday'],a:0,e:'He led Dhuhr, Asr, Maghrib, Isha and Fajr in Mina, then waited for sunrise before moving on. The four-rakah prayers were shortened to two; whether a pilgrim who has stayed long in Makkah also shortens differs between the schools.',src:'Muslim 1218 · Bukhari 1653 · Bukhari 1655'},
{q:'“Hajj is ___,” said the Prophet ﷺ.',o:['Mina','Arafah','Tawaf','Muzdalifah'],a:1,e:'“Hajj is Arafah.” Being there — on the 9th, or in the night before Fajr of the 10th — is the pillar without which there is no Hajj.',src:'Tirmidhi 889'},
{q:'True or false: the standing at Arafah is valid only on Jabal ar-Rahmah.',tf:1,a:1,e:'False — “I stood here, and all of Arafah is a place of standing.” Sitting in your tent inside the boundary counts in full; climbing the hill is not a rite.',src:'Muslim 1218 · Abu Dawud 1936'},
{q:'At Arafah, the Prophet ﷺ prayed Dhuhr and Asr…',o:['Each in its own time, four rakahs each','Together at Dhuhr time, two rakahs each, one adhan and two iqamahs','Together at Asr time, with an adhan for each','Not at all — he gave the whole day to dua'],a:1,e:'One adhan, two iqamahs, nothing prayed between them — then the standing began. That is the Sunnah and the majority practice; Abu Hanifa allowed the combining only behind the imam, so many Hanafi groups pray each in its own time in the tents, and the schools differ on who shortens — follow your group’s scholar.',src:'Muslim 1218 · Bukhari 1662'},
{q:'Did the Prophet ﷺ fast on the day of Arafah during his Hajj?',o:['Yes — he fasted, as on every day of Arafah','No — he drank milk in front of the people','Yes, but only until the Dhuhr prayer','He fasted both the 8th and the 9th'],a:1,e:'Umm al-Fadl sent him a bowl of milk and he drank it on his camel at Arafah. Most scholars say the pilgrim does not fast that day, keeping strength for dua; the famous fast of Arafah is for those not on Hajj.',src:'Bukhari 1988 · Bukhari 1989'},
{q:'“The best dua is the dua of the Day of Arafah, and the best that I and the prophets before me said is…”',o:['Subhan Allah wa bihamdihi, subhan Allah al-‘Azim','La ilaha illallah wahdahu la sharika lah, lahul-mulk wa lahul-hamd, wa huwa ‘ala kulli shay’in qadir','Astaghfirullah alladhi la ilaha illa huwa al-Hayy al-Qayyum wa atubu ilayh','Hasbunallahu wa ni‘mal-wakil, ni‘mal-mawla wa ni‘man-nasir'],a:1,e:'The words of tawhid — “None has the right to be worshipped but Allah alone, without partner; His is the dominion and His the praise, and He is over all things capable.”',src:'Tirmidhi 3585 (hasan — al-Albani) · al-Muwatta’ (mursal of Talhah ibn ‘Ubaydullah ibn Kariz)'},
{q:'“This day I have perfected for you your religion” (Quran 5:3) was revealed…',o:['On the day of Arafah, during the Farewell Hajj','On Laylat al-Qadr, in the cave of Hira','On the day of the Conquest of Makkah','On the morning after the battle of Badr'],a:0,e:'Umar (RA): “We know when and where it was revealed to the Prophet ﷺ: it was a Friday, and he was standing at Arafah.”',src:'Bukhari 45 · Quran 5:3'},
{q:'“There is no day on which Allah frees more servants from the Fire than…”',o:['The day of Arafah','Eid al-Fitr','The first of Muharram','Any Friday'],a:0,e:'“…the day of Arafah. He draws near, then praises them before the angels: What do these want?” Bring your dua list — and your family’s.',src:'Muslim 1348'},
{q:'When did the Prophet ﷺ leave Arafah?',o:['Straight after praying Dhuhr and Asr','After sunset, once the disc of the sun had gone','At midnight, after resting in the tent','After praying Fajr there on the 10th'],a:1,e:'“He kept standing there till the sun set… and the disc of the sun had disappeared,” then moved calmly to Muzdalifah. Stay inside Arafah until sunset; the schools differ on what is owed by one who leaves early.',src:'Muslim 1218'},
{q:'A pilgrim’s bus is stuck and reaches Arafah at 1 a.m., the night before the 10th. Has he caught the standing?',o:['No — the time for the standing ended at sunset','Yes — reaching Arafah before Fajr on the 10th catches it','Only if he stays at Arafah until noon on the 10th','Only once he has paid a dam for arriving late'],a:1,e:'“Hajj is Arafah. Whoever comes on the night of Jam’, before Fajr, has caught the Hajj.” He stands for a moment, then goes on to Muzdalifah.',src:'Tirmidhi 889'},
{q:'Before Islam, Quraysh stopped at Muzdalifah instead of Arafah. Quran 2:199 commands…',o:['Depart from where all the people depart — stand at Arafah with everyone','Stay at Muzdalifah, as Quraysh had always done','Stand in Mina, closer to the House','Let each tribe keep its own place of standing'],a:0,e:'Aishah (RA): Quraysh (al-Hums) stayed at Muzdalifah while the other Arabs stood at Arafah; with Islam, Allah ordered His Prophet ﷺ to go to Arafah, stand there and depart from there.',src:'Bukhari 4520 · Quran 2:199'}
]},
{key:'h-nahr',short:'Muzdalifah & the 10th',j:'hajj',icon:'🌙',mk:'moon',name:'Muzdalifah & the 10th',desc:'The night, the pebbles, the order of the Day of Nahr & the first release',study:{tab:'umrah',sub:'steps',id:'sec-hd10'},rites:true,qs:'undefined',qs:[
{q:'Which place does the Quran call al-Mash‘ar al-Haram?',o:['Arafah','Muzdalifah','Mina','The mataf'],a:1,e:'“When you depart from Arafat, remember Allah at al-Mash‘ar al-Haram” — at Muzdalifah (also called Jam’), where the Prophet ﷺ stood in dua after Fajr until it was very bright.',src:'Quran 2:198 · Muslim 1218'},
{q:'At Muzdalifah, the Prophet ﷺ prayed Maghrib and Isha…',o:['On the road, each at its time, before arriving','Together on arrival — Maghrib three rakahs, Isha two','Each in its own time after arriving there','Only Maghrib that night, and Isha at Fajr time'],a:1,e:'On arrival he prayed Maghrib (three rakahs) and Isha (two) together, with no voluntary prayer between them, then lay down until dawn. Jabir (RA) reports one adhan and two iqamahs; Ibn Umar and Ibn Mas’ud (RA) describe the calls a little differently, and the schools follow different reports.',src:'Muslim 1218 · Muslim 1288 · Bukhari 1673'},
{q:'True or false: the Prophet ﷺ let the weak leave Muzdalifah during the night, ahead of the crowds.',tf:1,a:0,e:'True — Sawdah (RA), a slow woman, asked and he allowed her to leave before the rush; Ibn Abbas was sent ahead with the weak of the family. The schools differ on the earliest time (Shafi’is and Hanbalis: after midnight) — follow your group.',src:'Bukhari 1681 · Bukhari 1678'},
{q:'Must the pebbles be picked up at Muzdalifah?',o:['Yes — pebbles from anywhere else do not count','No — pebbles picked up elsewhere count too; his were picked on the morning of the 10th','Yes, and all seventy must be picked there that night','Only pebbles carried from Arafah count'],a:1,e:'On the morning of the 10th he said to Ibn Abbas, “Pick up some pebbles for me,” and he told the people to take pebbles as he came into Muhassir. Many pick up seven at Muzdalifah; pebbles from anywhere will do — just not ones already thrown at the jamarat, which some schools do not count.',src:'Nasai 3057 · Muslim 1282'},
{q:'How big should the pebbles be?',o:['About a chickpea or a date stone — fingertip size','About a walnut — between thumb and finger','About an egg, so that it surely reaches','About a fist, to show strength'],a:0,e:'“Like these. And beware of going to extremes in religion, for those before you were destroyed by going to extremes.”',src:'Nasai 3057 · Ibn Majah 3029 · Muslim 1299'},
{q:'On the 10th of Dhul Hijjah, which jamarah is stoned?',o:['All three','The small one only','Jamrat al-Aqabah only','The middle one only'],a:2,e:'Only the big pillar, Jamrat al-Aqabah, on the 10th — seven pebbles, a takbir with each. All three are stoned on the days that follow.',src:'Muslim 1218 · Bukhari 1750'},
{q:'The sunnah order of the Day of Nahr (the 10th) is…',o:['Tawaf → stoning → sacrifice → shave','Stoning → sacrifice → shave → tawaf al-ifadah','Shave → stoning → tawaf → sacrifice','Sacrifice → tawaf → stoning → shave'],a:1,e:'Anas (RA): he came to the Jamrah and stoned it, went to his place in Mina and sacrificed, then called the barber — and then rode to the House. Ifrad has no hady: stone, cut, tawaf.',src:'Muslim 1305 · Muslim 1218'},
{q:'On the 10th, people asked the Prophet ﷺ about doing these acts out of order — shaving before the sacrifice, sacrificing before stoning. He said…',o:['“Your Hajj is void”','“Do it, and there is no harm”','“Repeat it next year”','“Fast ten days”'],a:1,e:'“Whenever he was asked about anything done before or after, he said: Do it, and there is no harm.” Shafi’is and Hanbalis say nothing is owed; Hanafis require a dam for some changes of order, and Malikis a fidyah for shaving before the stoning and a dam for a tawaf al-ifadah done before it.',src:'Bukhari 1736 · Bukhari 1734'},
{q:'Where may the hady be slaughtered?',o:['Only back home, after the Hajj is over','Anywhere in Mina, or elsewhere in the Haram','Only on the plain of Arafah','Only in the courtyard beside the Kaaba'],a:1,e:'“I sacrificed here, and the whole of Mina is a place of sacrifice” — and “all the passes of Makkah are a place of sacrifice.” It must be within the Haram.',src:'Muslim 1218 · Abu Dawud 1937'},
{ht:['tamattu','qiran'],q:'A Tamattu’ or Qiran pilgrim cannot afford the hady. Quran 2:196 says…',o:['Pay for two sacrifices on the next trip','Fast three days during Hajj and seven on returning — ten in all','Nothing — the hady falls away completely','Do an extra Umrah from Tan’eem instead'],a:1,e:'“Whoever cannot find one — a fast of three days during Hajj and of seven when you have returned. Those are ten complete.” Which days count differs a little by school — ask before the 8th.',src:'Quran 2:196'},
{q:'After the stoning and the shave on the 10th, which ONE of these is still forbidden — in all four schools?',o:['Normal clothes','Cutting the nails','Marital intimacy','Wearing perfume'],a:2,e:'Aishah (RA) perfumed him “for his release, when he came out of ihram, before he performed tawaf” — that is, before tawaf al-ifadah. Clothes, nails and perfume return at this first release; intimacy waits for tawaf al-ifadah — and, for most schools, the sa’i too if you still owe it. (Malikis also keep hunting forbidden and dislike perfume until then; Malikis, Shafi’is and Hanbalis keep the marriage contract forbidden.)',src:'Bukhari 1754 · Bukhari 1539'},
{ht:['tamattu'],q:'Tamattu’: the companions who had done Umrah first did the sa’i of their Hajj…',o:['Never — the sa’i of their Umrah covered it','After returning from Mina, with tawaf al-ifadah','At Marwah on the 8th, with no tawaf before it','At Muzdalifah, on the night of the 10th'],a:1,e:'Aishah (RA): those who had done Umrah first “performed another tawaf (between Safa and Marwah) after returning from Mina.” The four schools require this second sa’i of the Tamattu’ pilgrim, normally after tawaf al-ifadah; a minority (e.g. Ibn Taymiyyah) held the first sa’i suffices.',src:'Bukhari 1556 · Bukhari 1572'},
{ht:['qiran','ifrad'],q:'Qiran or Ifrad: you did the sa’i right after your arrival tawaf. For the majority, after tawaf al-ifadah you…',o:['Repeat it, since the first came before Arafah','Do not repeat it — that sa’i counts for your Hajj','Do two more, one for each tawaf','Owe a dam for doing it so early'],a:1,e:'Jabir (RA): the Prophet ﷺ and his companions — meaning those who, like him, stayed in ihram — went between Safa and Marwah only once: “his first tawaf.” Hanafis hold that a Qiran pilgrim does a second sa’i; follow your school.',src:'Muslim 1215'}
]},
{key:'h-tashreeq',short:'Tashreeq & farewell',j:'hajj',icon:'🪨',mk:'stone',name:'Tashreeq & the farewell',desc:'The three jamarat, leaving Mina & tawaf al-wada',study:{tab:'umrah',sub:'steps',id:'sec-hd11'},rites:true,qs:'undefined',qs:[
{q:'The days of tashreeq are…',o:['The 8th to the 10th','The 11th, 12th and 13th of Dhul Hijjah','The last ten nights of Ramadan','The first three days of Muharram'],a:1,e:'“The days of tashreeq are days of eating, drinking and the remembrance of Allah” — the “numbered days” of Quran 2:203.',src:'Muslim 1141 · Quran 2:203'},
{q:'On the 11th and 12th, in which order did the Prophet ﷺ stone the three jamarat?',o:['Aqabah → middle → small (nearest Masjid al-Khayf)','Small (nearest Masjid al-Khayf) → middle → Aqabah','Middle → small → Aqabah, then the dua','Any order, as long as each gets seven'],a:1,e:'The small one by Masjid al-Khayf, then the middle, then Aqabah — “I saw the Prophet ﷺ doing like this.” Most scholars make this order a condition; Hanafis hold it is a sunnah.',src:'Bukhari 1751 · Bukhari 1753'},
{q:'How many pebbles are thrown at each jamarah?',o:['3','5','7','10'],a:2,e:'Seven, one at a time, with a takbir for each.',src:'Bukhari 1753'},
{q:'When did the Prophet ﷺ stone on the days of tashreeq?',o:['Before Fajr, to beat the crowds','In the early morning, soon after sunrise','After the sun had passed its zenith (zawal)','Only after Maghrib, at night'],a:2,e:'He stoned on the 10th in the forenoon, and afterwards “when the sun had declined.” Ibn Umar: “We used to wait till the sun declined.” Whether you may stone after sunset differs: Hanafis and Shafi’is allow it, and the Saudi fatwa bodies follow this; the Hanbali school moves it to the next day after midday — follow your group.',src:'Muslim 1299 · Bukhari 1746'},
{q:'On the 11th and 12th, after which jamarat do you stop for a long dua?',o:['After all three, facing the qibla','After the small and the middle only','After Jamrat al-Aqabah only','After none — he left each one at once'],a:1,e:'Takbir with each pebble, then a long dua facing the qibla after the small and the middle — and none after Aqabah.',src:'Bukhari 1753'},
{q:'Leaving Mina on the 12th, how many pebbles will you have thrown in all?',o:['21','49','63','70'],a:1,e:'7 at Aqabah on the 10th + 21 on the 11th + 21 on the 12th = 49. Staying for the 13th adds 21 more: 70.',src:'Muslim 1218 · Bukhari 1753'},
{q:'True or false: for the majority, a pilgrim who is still in Mina when the sun sets on the 12th stays the night and stones on the 13th.',tf:1,a:0,e:'True — Ibn Umar (RA): “Whoever is at Mina when the sun sets on the middle day of tashreeq must not leave until he has stoned the jamarat the next day.” The verse gives the choice — “whoever hastens in two days, there is no sin on him.” Hanafis allow leaving that night, before Fajr of the 13th; and many scholars, including Ibn Baz, excuse one who had already set off and was held up in Mina by the traffic.',src:'Quran 2:203 · Ibn Umar in al-Muwatta’ (Hajj; mawquf, sahih) · Tirmidhi 889'},
{q:'Whom did the Prophet ﷺ excuse from spending the nights of Mina in Mina?',o:['Anyone who prefers to sleep in a Makkah hotel','Al-Abbas, to give pilgrims water, and the camel herders','Only the elderly and the sick of his family','No one — not even his own household'],a:1,e:'Al-Abbas (RA) asked to stay in Makkah for the siqayah and was allowed; the herders were allowed to stay with the camels and gather their stoning. Scholars extend this to others with a real need.',src:'Bukhari 1745 · Abu Dawud 1975'},
{q:'“None of you should depart until his last act is at the House” — this is about…',o:['Tawaf al-qudum, the tawaf of arrival','Tawaf al-ifadah, the pillar of the 10th','Tawaf al-wada, the farewell tawaf','The sa’i between Safa and Marwah'],a:2,e:'The farewell tawaf: the last thing you do in Makkah before you travel home.',src:'Muslim 1327 · Bukhari 1755'},
{q:'Who is excused from the farewell tawaf?',o:['Anyone whose flight leaves early','A woman still on her period when she leaves','Every pilgrim over the age of sixty','Every Qiran and Ifrad pilgrim'],a:1,e:'“…except the menstruating women, who were excused.” Safiyyah (RA) could leave because she had already done tawaf al-ifadah. People who live in Makkah do not owe it either.',src:'Bukhari 1755 · Bukhari 1757'},
{q:'What follows the farewell tawaf?',o:['A last sa’i between Safa and Marwah','Two rakahs, then you set off','One more stoning of Aqabah','A second shave or trim'],a:1,e:'Seven rounds in ordinary clothes, no raml and no sa’i; then the two rakahs prayed after every tawaf, and you leave. The Prophet ﷺ did his at night, after a short sleep at al-Muhassab, before setting out for Madinah.',src:'Quran 2:125 · Bukhari 1691 · Bukhari 1764'},
{q:'True or false: a woman whose period starts before tawaf al-ifadah may leave without it, just as with the farewell tawaf.',tf:1,a:1,e:'False — tawaf al-ifadah is a pillar. “Would she delay us?” he asked about Safiyyah, until told she had already done it. She waits until she is pure; if her travel truly cannot wait, she asks a scholar before leaving. Only the farewell is waived.',src:'Bukhari 1757 · Bukhari 1650'}
]},
{key:'h-fiqh',short:'Hajj fiqh',j:'hajj',icon:'⚖️',mk:'book',name:'Hajj fiqh — mistakes & concessions',desc:'Pillars vs wajibs, dam, fidyah & the ease given to the weak',study:{sub:'learn',id:'qaCard'},qs:'undefined',qs:[
{q:'Which of these is a pillar of Hajj that no dam can replace?',o:['The night at Muzdalifah','Tawaf al-ifadah','The farewell tawaf','Stoning the jamarat'],a:1,e:'Tawaf al-ifadah is a pillar: the Hajj stays incomplete until it is done. For the majority the other three are wajibs, repaired by a dam if missed (Malikis count the farewell tawaf a sunnah).',src:'Quran 22:29'},
{q:'A pilgrim misses the standing at Arafah altogether — he arrives after Fajr on the 10th. What follows?',o:['He pays a dam and continues to Muzdalifah','The Hajj is missed: he leaves ihram by an Umrah and repeats Hajj later','He stands at Muzdalifah instead, which counts for Arafah','Nothing is owed, since Arafah is a sunnah'],a:1,e:'“Hajj is Arafah” — no dam replaces it. He leaves ihram by doing the acts of an Umrah (tawaf, sa’i and the cut) and performs the Hajj in another year; this is reported as Umar’s (RA) ruling for Companions who arrived on the Day of Sacrifice. The majority add a sacrifice; Hanafis owe none.',src:'Tirmidhi 889 · Umar’s ruling, al-Muwatta’ (Hajj; mawquf — broken chain: Sulayman ibn Yasar from Umar)'},
{q:'Lice forced a man in ihram to shave his head. Quran 2:196 and the Prophet ﷺ set a fidyah of…',o:['Nothing, since an illness excuses it','Fasting three days, or feeding six poor people, or a sheep','Repeating the whole Hajj the following year','Sacrificing a camel in the Haram'],a:1,e:'Ka’b ibn Ujrah was told: “Have your head shaved, then either fast three days, or feed six poor persons, or slaughter a sheep.”',src:'Bukhari 1814 · Quran 2:196'},
{q:'Killing game while in ihram is compensated by…',o:['An apology and a prayer of repentance only','An equivalent animal judged by two just men — or feeding the poor, or fasting','Nothing the first time, and a sheep the second','Leaving ihram and entering it again at the miqat'],a:1,e:'“…the penalty is an equivalent from sacrificial animals to what he killed, as judged by two just men… or an expiation: feeding needy people, or the equivalent of that in fasting.”',src:'Quran 5:95'},
{q:'A pilgrim is too ill to reach the jamarat. He may…',o:['Skip the stoning with nothing owed','Appoint a fellow pilgrim to throw on his behalf','Throw all seventy pebbles at once on the 13th','Throw them from the hotel'],a:1,e:'All four schools allow the one unable to delegate the stoning; the deputy normally throws his own pebbles first. Malikis add that the one unable still owes a dam.',src:'Quran 64:16 · Ibn Majah 3038 (weak chain; the practice is agreed)'},
{q:'Which of these is NOT a concession given to the weak?',o:['Leaving Muzdalifah during the night','Having someone else throw the pebbles','Doing tawaf carried or in a wheelchair','Skipping tawaf al-ifadah'],a:3,e:'Sawdah left Muzdalifah early, and the Prophet ﷺ himself did tawaf mounted. Tawaf al-ifadah can be done late, on the upper floors or in a wheelchair — but never skipped.',src:'Bukhari 1681 · Bukhari 1607'},
{q:'Your wudu breaks in round 5 of tawaf al-ifadah. What do you do?',o:['Your Hajj is invalid and must be repeated','Renew the wudu and return to the tawaf','Carry on, as wudu is only recommended','Leave the tawaf until next year’s Hajj'],a:1,e:'Renew your wudu and return. Hanafis and Shafi’is build on the rounds already done (resume the interrupted round, or repeat it to be safe); Malikis and Hanbalis restart from round 1 — the safer course if you are able. Nothing is lost either way.',src:'Tirmidhi 960'},
{q:'On the 9th you realise you put on a stitched shirt by mistake an hour ago. What do you do first?',o:['Give up — the Hajj is spoiled now','Take it off at once, carry on, then ask about any fidyah','Keep it on, since the harm is already done','Leave ihram and enter it again at the miqat'],a:1,e:'A man in a perfumed cloak was simply told to take it off and wash off the scent. Forgetting is not a sin; whether a fidyah is still owed differs — Shafi’is and Hanbalis excuse clothing and perfume, Hanafis and Malikis do not.',src:'Bukhari 1536 · Quran 2:286'},
{q:'Which violation is the only one that can spoil the Hajj itself?',o:['Wearing a watch or a ring','Marital intimacy before the first release','Forgetting the talbiyah for a whole day','Losing a pebble before throwing it'],a:1,e:'“…no sexual relations… during Hajj.” Before Arafah it spoils the Hajj in all four schools — the Hajj is still completed and then repeated another year; after Arafah the schools differ. Every other violation is repaired by a fidyah, or by nothing.',src:'Quran 2:197'},
{q:'A pilgrim in ihram is blocked from reaching the House. Quran 2:196 says…',o:['Stay in ihram until the following year','Offer whatever hady is easy, then leave ihram','Go home at once — nothing is owed','Fast sixty days in a row'],a:1,e:'“If you are prevented, then [offer] what can be obtained with ease of sacrificial animals.” At Hudaybiyyah the Prophet ﷺ slaughtered his hady and shaved. The schools differ on the details — whether illness counts as being prevented, and what must be made up afterwards — so ask a scholar.',src:'Quran 2:196 · Bukhari 1809'},
{q:'True or false: tawaf al-ifadah done after the days of tashreeq — because a period kept you waiting — is still valid.',tf:1,a:0,e:'True — a pillar is never “too late”: the Hajj is completed when it is done. With an excuse such as a period, nothing is owed. Delaying it without an excuse is discouraged, and some schools then owe a dam (Hanafis, once the days of sacrifice have passed; Malikis, once Dhul Hijjah ends).',src:'Bukhari 1757 · Quran 22:29'},
{q:'After her Hajj, Aishah (RA) — whose period had kept her from her Umrah — performed an Umrah from…',o:['Dhul-Hulayfah','Tan’eem','Arafah','Ji’ranah'],a:1,e:'The Prophet ﷺ sent her with her brother Abdur-Rahman to Tan’eem: “This Umrah is instead of your missed one.” Today Masjid Aisha stands there.',src:'Bukhari 1556 · Bukhari 1784'}
]}];
