/* ════════════════════════ HAJJ ════════════════════════
   Hajj is a superset of Umrah: Part 1 (ihram → tawaf → sa’i → halq) is RITES above; Part 2 (8–13 Dhul Hijjah + the farewell tawaf) is HAJJ_RITES.
   Each step: req 'rukn'|'wajib'|'sunnah'; miss = what to do if it was missed (always ends with "tell your group leader / ask a scholar");
   ht = the Hajj types the step applies to (absent = all); v = per-type overrides merged over b/p; del = may be delegated; counter = which counter to open.
   Rulings follow the majority view and name the differences where they matter. The content has not yet had a scholar’s review — the app says so in the Hajj header. */
var HAJJ_TYPES={
tamattu:{t:'Tamattu’',s:'Umrah first, exit ihram, then Hajj on 8 Dhul Hijjah',talb:'Labbayk Allahumma ‘Umrah',ar:'لَبَّيْكَ اللَّهُمَّ عُمْرَةً',hady:true,d:'The way the Prophet ﷺ told his companions without a sacrificial animal to do it (Bukhari 1572, 1651): Umrah on arrival, a few free days in Makkah, then ihram again for Hajj. The easiest for the family and the one most packages assume. A hady is owed.'},
qiran:{t:'Qiran',s:'Umrah and Hajj in one ihram',talb:'Labbayk Allahumma ‘Umratan wa Hajjan',ar:'لَبَّيْكَ اللَّهُمَّ عُمْرَةً وَحَجًّا',hady:true,d:'One ihram carries both — most scholars hold this was the Prophet’s ﷺ own Hajj (Bukhari 1551, 1691), though some reports describe it as Ifrad (Bukhari 1562). You do the arrival tawaf and sa’i but do not cut your hair — you stay in ihram from the miqat until the 10th. A hady is owed.'},
ifrad:{t:'Ifrad',s:'Hajj alone',talb:'Labbayk Allahumma Hajjan',ar:'لَبَّيْكَ اللَّهُمَّ حَجًّا',hady:false,d:'Hajj on its own, no Umrah joined to it. Arrival tawaf and sa’i, then you stay in ihram until the 10th. No hady is owed (an udhiyah is optional — a sunnah for most schools; Malikis do not prescribe it for the pilgrim, and the Hanafi school makes it obligatory for a pilgrim who counts as resident and has the means).'}};

/* Gregorian date of 8 Dhul Hijjah (Umm al-Qura). Verified against aladhan hToG (calendarMethod=UAQ) and the hijridate Umm al-Qura table for 1447–1460; Intl ‘islamic-umalqura’ (current Chrome and Node) is a day late for 1454, 1456, 1457 and 1458 (for 1454 it puts 8 Dhul Hijjah on 10 March 2033), so hajjD8() takes this table first. Published Umm al-Qura Eid al-Adha dates (10 DH) are 27 May 2026, 16 May 2027, 5 May 2028, 24 Apr 2029. Moon sighting can move the real day by one — ST.hajjShift covers that. */
var HAJJ_D8={1447:'2026-05-25',1448:'2027-05-14',1449:'2028-05-03',1450:'2029-04-22',1451:'2030-04-11',1452:'2031-03-31',1453:'2032-03-20',1454:'2033-03-09',1455:'2034-02-27',1456:'2035-02-16',1457:'2036-02-05',1458:'2037-01-24',1459:'2038-01-14',1460:'2039-01-03'};

var HAJJ_RITES=[
{id:'hd8',hd:8,ico:'⛺',title:'8 Dhul Hijjah · Tarwiyah — ihram for Hajj & Mina',sub:'Ihram from where you are staying · Mina · five prayers, the four-rakah ones shortened',hn:'"Learn your rites, for I do not know whether I will perform Hajj after this one." — Abu Dawud 1970 (also in Muslim)',steps:[
{id:'h8ghusl',req:'sunnah',ht:['tamattu'],why:'The Prophet ﷺ bathed before ihram (Tirmidhi 830) and was perfumed by Aishah (RA) before assuming it (Bukhari 1539). On the 8th his companions who had done Umrah assumed ihram for Hajj from where they were staying in Makkah (Muslim 1214, 1218).',b:'Ghusl, perfume on the body, ihram garments — from your hotel',p:'Morning of the 8th (or the night before, if your bus leaves early). Shower, trim nails, men perfume the body not the cloth, put on the izar and rida. Women: ordinary modest clothing. There is no miqat for this ihram — you assume it where you are staying in Makkah, Aziziyah included.',miss:'Forgot the ghusl or the perfume? Nothing is owed — both are sunnah. Just make the intention and carry on. If in doubt, tell your group leader or ask a scholar.'},
{id:'h8niyyah',req:'rukn',ht:['tamattu'],kid:'white',why:'Ihram is the pillar that opens the Hajj — "actions are but by intentions" (Bukhari 1). The companions did exactly this on the Day of Tarwiyah: "we assumed ihram for Hajj from al-Abtah" (Jabir, Muslim 1214).',b:'Make the intention for Hajj',p:'Say it with your heart and tongue before you set off for Mina. From this moment the ihram rules apply again until the 10th. If you fear illness or an obstacle, add the condition (ishtirat) — see your Umrah ihram step.',dua:{ar:'لَبَّيْكَ اللَّهُمَّ حَجًّا',tl:'Labbayk Allahumma Hajjan',tr:'Here I am, O Allah, for Hajj.'},miss:'Not yet in ihram and already in Mina or Arafah? Make the intention now, wherever you are — the Hajj is valid as long as you are in ihram before your standing at Arafah ends; whether a dam is owed for assuming it outside Makkah differs between the schools (the Hanbali school: none; others require one, some only outside the Haram boundary, which Mina is inside). If Fajr of the 10th comes and you never entered ihram, there is no Hajj this year. Tell your group leader / ask a scholar.'},
{id:'h8stay',req:'rukn',ht:['qiran','ifrad'],why:'Those who had a sacrificial animal with them did not leave ihram after the arrival tawaf and sa’i: "I cannot finish my ihram till I slaughter my hady" (Bukhari 1725).',b:'You are already in ihram — stay in it',v:{qiran:{p:'Your ihram from the miqat covers both Umrah and Hajj. No new intention, no new ghusl is required (a shower without scent is fine). Keep the talbiyah going and head to Mina with your group.'},ifrad:{p:'Your ihram from the miqat is the ihram of Hajj. No new intention is needed (a shower without scent is fine). Keep the talbiyah going and head to Mina with your group.'}},p:'Your ihram from the miqat still stands. Keep the talbiyah going and head to Mina with your group.',miss:'If you cut your hair or changed into ordinary clothes after the arrival tawaf by mistake, your ihram still stands — put the ihram back on, stop the act, and carry on. Whether a fidyah is owed differs: Shafi’is and Hanbalis excuse perfume and clothing done forgetfully or unknowingly, but not cutting hair or nails; Hanafis and Malikis require the fidyah regardless; some Hanbalis and many contemporary scholars say nothing is owed for anything done forgetfully or unknowingly (Bukhari 1536 — a man in a perfumed cloak was simply told to remove it). The fidyah is a fast of 3 days, feeding 6 poor, or a sheep (Quran 2:196; the amounts are from the hadith of Ka’b ibn Ujrah, Bukhari 1814). Tell your group leader / ask a scholar whether one applies to you.'},
{id:'h8talb',req:'sunnah',rep:'Until the first pebble at Aqabah',why:'The Prophet ﷺ "kept on reciting the talbiyah till he did the rami of Jamrat al-Aqabah" (Bukhari 1685).',b:'Recite the talbiyah abundantly',p:'Men aloud, women softly — in the bus, in the tent, on the walk. It only stops on the 10th at Jamrat al-Aqabah: most scholars say at the first pebble; others continue to the last. Either is fine (Malikis stop it on arriving at Arafah after midday on the 9th).',dua:{ar:'لَبَّيْكَ اللَّهُمَّ لَبَّيْكَ، لَبَّيْكَ لَا شَرِيكَ لَكَ لَبَّيْكَ، إِنَّ الْحَمْدَ وَالنِّعْمَةَ لَكَ وَالْمُلْكَ، لَا شَرِيكَ لَكَ',tl:'Labbayk Allahumma labbayk, labbayka la sharika laka labbayk, innal-hamda wan-ni’mata laka wal-mulk, la sharika lak',tr:'Here I am, O Allah, here I am. Here I am, You have no partner, here I am. All praise, favour and sovereignty are Yours. You have no partner.'}},
{id:'h8mina',req:'sunnah',why:'Anas (RA) was asked where the Prophet ﷺ prayed Dhuhr and Asr on the Day of Tarwiyah: "at Mina" (Bukhari 1653). He prayed two rakahs there (Bukhari 1655, 1656).',b:'Go to Mina · pray Dhuhr, Asr, Maghrib, Isha and Fajr there — each at its time, the four-rakah ones shortened',p:'Most groups move by bus (some walk) in the morning or afternoon — the Mashaer train links only Mina, Muzdalifah and Arafah, not Makkah itself; find your camp and maktab number before you do anything else. Four-rakah prayers are prayed as two, each at its own time, in your tent or at Masjid al-Khayf. Shortening is for travellers: Malikis shorten in Mina and Arafah regardless (as do many Saudi scholars today); Hanafis (15+ days in Makkah before the 8th) count you a resident and pray four rakahs here; Shafi’is count you a resident after 4 full days in Makkah (not counting the days of arrival and departure), but let you shorten from the moment you leave for Mina if you will set off for home after the rites without another 4 full days in Makkah (al-Majmu’ 8/88); Hanbalis shorten unless you will stay on in Makkah for more than 4 days after the Hajj — ask your group leader which your group follows. Spend the day in talbiyah, Quran and rest — tomorrow is the big day.',miss:'Your package took you straight to Arafah, or you reached Mina only at night? Nothing is owed — the day and night in Mina are sunnah, not a pillar. Rest, keep up the talbiyah, and be in Arafah tomorrow. If in doubt, tell your group leader / ask a scholar.'},
{id:'h8night',req:'sunnah',why:'He ﷺ stayed in Mina until the sun rose on the 9th, then moved to Arafah (Muslim 1218).',b:'Sleep in Mina · leave for Arafah after sunrise',p:'Drink water, charge your phone, put tomorrow’s essentials (ihram spare, umbrella, dua list) in your day-bag. Go to bed early. In the morning, after Fajr and sunrise, move to Arafah with your group saying the talbiyah and the takbir.'}
]},
{id:'hd9',hd:9,ico:'🏔️',title:'9 Dhul Hijjah · Arafah',sub:'The pillar of Hajj · Dhuhr + Asr combined · wuquf until sunset',hn:'"Hajj is Arafah." — Tirmidhi 889',kn:'<b>With children:</b> stay inside the tent during the midday heat · the standing is valid sitting, lying down or asleep · let them hold your hand while you make dua · leave for the buses only when the group leader says.',steps:[
{id:'h9arrive',req:'sunnah',why:'"All of Arafah is a place of standing" (Muslim 1218, Abu Dawud 1936); the exclusion of the hollow of Uranah comes from Ibn Majah 3012 (weak chain) and the Muwatta, and the schools agree on it. The Prophet ﷺ camped at Namirah until the sun declined, then moved into Arafah (Muslim 1218).',b:'Arrive and check you are inside Arafah',p:'Large signs mark the boundary; Wadi Uranah and part of Masjid Namirah lie outside it. Your camp will be inside — but if you walk, stay within the signs. Rest in the shade until Dhuhr; do not fast today — the Prophet ﷺ drank milk at Arafah in front of the people (Bukhari 1988, 1989).',miss:'If you discover you spent the whole day outside the boundary (in Uranah), move inside now — any moment inside Arafah between Dhuhr of the 9th (Hanbalis: from Fajr of the 9th) and Fajr of the 10th makes the wuquf valid (Malikis: it must include a moment after sunset, so stay inside until the sun has set). Tell your group leader / ask a scholar.'},
{id:'h9salah',req:'sunnah',why:'He ﷺ prayed Dhuhr and Asr together at Dhuhr time with one adhan and two iqamahs (Jabir, Muslim 1218), shortened to two rakahs each. Salim told al-Hajjaj, and Ibn Umar (RA) confirmed it: "if you want to follow the sunnah, pray early, just after midday, on the Day of Arafah" (Bukhari 1662).',b:'Dhuhr + Asr together at Dhuhr time, two rakahs each',p:'Pray behind the imam at Namirah if you are there, otherwise in the tent with your group: one adhan, two iqamahs, nothing in between. Then the standing begins. Hanafis combine the two only when praying behind the Hajj imam (otherwise each at its own time), and a pilgrim their school counts as a resident of Makkah (15+ days; Shafi’is: 4 full days, not counting arrival and departure, and staying on 4 full days after the Hajj; Hanbalis: if staying on in Makkah more than 4 days after the Hajj) prays four rakahs each — in the relied-upon Shafi’i view such a resident also prays each at its own time, since the combining is tied to travel — ask your group leader.',miss:'Prayed them separately or at Asr time? Both prayers are valid; combining is the sunnah, not an obligation. Nothing is owed. Tell your group leader if you are unsure what the group is doing next.'},
{id:'h9wuquf',req:'rukn',why:'"Hajj is Arafah. Whoever comes [to Arafah] on the night of Jam’ (the night before the 10th), before Fajr, has caught the Hajj" (Tirmidhi 889). He ﷺ stood facing the qibla, hands raised, until the sun set (Nasai 3011, Muslim 1218).',b:'Stand at Arafah — from Dhuhr until sunset',p:'This is the Hajj. Face the qibla, raise your hands and talk to your Lord: istighfar, dua for yourself, your parents, your list, the ummah. Sitting, lying down, in a wheelchair or a bus, inside the tent — it all counts; there is no need to climb Jabal ar-Rahmah. Menstruating sisters stand like everyone else — purity is not required. Pace yourself: water, shade, a little Quran, then dua again.',miss:'If you were not inside Arafah at any moment between Dhuhr on the 9th (Hanbalis: from Fajr of the 9th) and Fajr on the 10th, there is no Hajj this year: you exit by doing the rites of Umrah (tawaf, sa’i, cut the hair) and make the Hajj up another year — a hady is owed according to the majority (Hanafis: none), and the Hajj is still obligatory on you. Arrived after sunset but before Fajr? Your wuquf is valid (Tirmidhi 889). Tell your group leader / ask a scholar before you do anything.'},
{id:'h9dua',req:'sunnah',rep:'Keep repeating until sunset',why:'"The best of supplication is the supplication of the Day of Arafah, and the best of what I and the prophets before me have said is: la ilaha illallah…" (Tirmidhi 3585).',b:'The dua of Arafah',p:'Say it slowly, again and again, with your own duas between. These hours of Arafah are among the most precious of the whole year — "there is no day on which Allah frees more people from the Fire than the Day of Arafah" (Sahih Muslim).',dua:{ar:'لَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ وَحْدَهُ لَا شَرِيكَ لَهُ، لَهُ الْمُلْكُ وَلَهُ الْحَمْدُ وَهُوَ عَلَىٰ كُلِّ شَيْءٍ قَدِيرٌ',tl:'La ilaha illallahu wahdahu la sharika lah, lahul-mulku wa lahul-hamdu wa huwa ’ala kulli shay’in qadir',tr:'None has the right to be worshipped but Allah alone, without partner. His is the dominion and His is the praise, and He is able to do all things.'}},
{id:'h9sunset',req:'wajib',why:'He ﷺ stayed "until the sun had set" and then departed (Muslim 1218), moving calmly, holding his camel back, telling the people: "calmness, calmness" (Muslim 1218).',b:'Stay until the sun has set, then leave calmly for Muzdalifah',p:'Do not join the rush before sunset. When the disc has gone, follow your group to the buses or walk — the path is marked. Maghrib is not prayed on the road: when asked, the Prophet ﷺ said "the prayer is ahead of you" (Bukhari 1667). Keep the talbiyah going.',miss:'Still in Arafah? Stay until the disc has gone — then nothing is owed. Left before sunset? Hanafis, Shafi’is and Hanbalis: the Hajj is valid. Hanafis and Hanbalis: a dam (a sheep, distributed in the Haram) is owed — Hanafis: returning before sunset removes it; Hanbalis: returning any time before Fajr removes it. Shafi’is (their sounder view): staying until sunset is only recommended, so the dam is recommended, not owed. Malikis: being inside Arafah for part of the night after sunset is a pillar — go back before Fajr or the Hajj is missed. Tell your group leader / ask a scholar.'}
]},
{id:'hd9n',hd:9,night:true,ico:'🌙',title:'Night before the 10th · Muzdalifah',sub:'Maghrib + Isha together · sleep · pebbles · the weak leave after midnight',hn:'"…remember Allah at al-Mash’ar al-Haram." — Quran 2:198',steps:[
{id:'h9nsalah',req:'sunnah',why:'He ﷺ "offered Maghrib and Isha together at al-Muzdalifah" (Bukhari 1674), with one adhan and two iqamahs and no sunnah prayers between them (Muslim 1218; Bukhari 1673).',b:'On arrival: Maghrib (3) + Isha (2) together, then nothing else',p:'Muzdalifah is wide ("all of Muzdalifah is a place of standing" — Abu Dawud 1936), but check the boundary signs where your bus stops; the valley of Muhassir is outside it. Pray as soon as you arrive — Maghrib in full, Isha shortened (four rakahs if your school counts you a resident of Makkah — see the 8th; in the relied-upon Shafi’i view such a resident also prays the two each at its own time), no nafl after. If you fear you will arrive only after midnight, many scholars (including the Saudi fatwa bodies) say pray both on the way before midnight; the four schools still count Isha as on time until Fajr when there is a need. Never let Fajr come without praying them.',miss:'Prayed Maghrib at Arafah or on the bus? For the majority the prayer is valid — combining here is the sunnah, not a condition of Hajj — and nothing is owed. Hanafis say a Maghrib prayed before Muzdalifah must be prayed again there before Fajr, so if your group is Hanafi, repeat it on arrival. Tell your group leader if you are unsure.'},
{id:'h9nstay',req:'wajib',why:'He ﷺ lay down until dawn, prayed Fajr early, then stood at al-Mash’ar al-Haram making takbir, tahlil and dua "until the sky became very bright", and left before sunrise (Muslim 1218). Allah says: "remember Allah at al-Mash’ar al-Haram" (Quran 2:198).',b:'Spend the night here · Fajr early · dhikr until the sky brightens · leave before sunrise',p:'Sleep on the ground under the sky — the Prophet ﷺ rested rather than staying up in night prayer (many scholars still advise not leaving the witr). Pray Fajr at its first moment, then face the qibla and make dhikr and dua until it is light. Leave for Mina before the sun rises, with the talbiyah.',miss:'Did not stay in Muzdalifah at all, or left before midnight without an excuse? The majority say a dam is owed (a sheep, slaughtered and given to the poor of the Haram); the Hajj is valid. Hanafis count the wajib as being there after Fajr, so leaving after midnight without excuse is also a dam for them. Those with a valid excuse (see the next step) owe nothing. Stuck in a bus queue and arrived only after Fajr? Scholars differ; most say nothing is owed if you did your best. Tell your group leader / ask a scholar.'},
{id:'h9nweak',req:'sunnah',why:'The Prophet ﷺ sent the weak of his family ahead on the night of Muzdalifah (Bukhari 1678); Ibn Umar (RA) would send them ahead by night (Bukhari 1676); Asma (RA) left when the moon set and stoned the Jamrah before Fajr (Bukhari 1679).',b:'Elderly, unwell, sisters and their carers: you may leave after midnight',p:'If standing in the crowds at dawn is beyond you — age, illness, pregnancy, small children, or you are the one escorting them — leave once the moon has set (on this night between about 2 and 4 am, in the last third of the night; Shafi’is and Hanbalis already allow leaving after midnight) and go to Mina. Leaving Muzdalifah after moonset is agreed for the weak; this is a concession the Prophet ﷺ gave, using it is not a shortcoming, and nothing is owed. The time of the stoning differs: Shafi’is and Hanbalis allow it from after midnight (Asma stoned before Fajr, Bukhari 1679); Hanafis and Malikis hold it is valid only from Fajr. Follow your group’s school; if unsure, wait for Fajr — it is valid for everyone.',miss:'Nothing to make up — this is a permission, not a duty. If you are unsure whether your excuse counts, tell your group leader / ask a scholar.'},
{id:'h9npebbles',req:'sunnah',why:'On the morning of Aqabah the Prophet ﷺ asked Ibn Abbas (RA) to pick up pebbles for him — small ones, "pebbles for flicking" (hasa al-khadhf, about the size of a chickpea or a date stone) — and said "like these; beware of going to extremes" (Nasai 3057, Ibn Majah 3029).',b:'Collect 7 small pebbles for tomorrow (49 or 70 if you want them all now)',p:'Chickpea-sized, from the ground anywhere — Muzdalifah, Mina or the road; there is nothing special about Muzdalifah stones, it is simply convenient. There is no need to wash them (the Prophet ﷺ is not reported to have done so; some scholars recommend it), and do not pick big ones. Keep them in a small pouch: 7 for the 10th, then 21 a day for the 11th and 12th (and the 13th if you stay).',miss:'No pebbles? Pick them up anywhere in Mina on your way to the Jamarat — the ground around the camps is full of them. Nothing is owed. If in doubt, tell your group leader / ask a scholar.'},
{id:'h9ndhikr',req:'sunnah',rep:'After Fajr until it is light',why:'"When you pour down from Arafat, remember Allah at al-Mash’ar al-Haram" (Quran 2:198). He ﷺ "made takbir, tahlil and dua" there (Muslim 1218).',b:'Dhikr and dua at al-Mash’ar al-Haram',p:'Stand facing the qibla after Fajr — by the mosque of al-Mash’ar if you are near it, otherwise where you slept. Takbir, tahlil, hamd, then your duas. This is the last of the great standing places — do not sleep through it.',dua:{ar:'اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ، اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ، لَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ، وَاللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ، اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ وَلِلَّهِ الْحَمْدُ',tl:'Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar, la ilaha illallah, wallahu akbar, Allahu akbar wa lillahil-hamd',tr:'Allah is the Greatest, Allah is the Greatest, there is no god but Allah; Allah is the Greatest, Allah is the Greatest, and to Allah belongs all praise. (This wording is the takbir of these days reported from Ibn Mas’ud (RA) — Ibn Abi Shaybah; the Prophet ﷺ made takbir and tahlil here, Muslim 1218)'}}
]},
{id:'hd10',hd:10,ico:'🕋',title:'10 Dhul Hijjah · Yawm an-Nahr',sub:'Rami of Aqabah · hady · halq · tawaf al-ifadah & sa’i · back to Mina',hn:'A man said, "I did not realise, and shaved before slaughtering." He ﷺ said: "Slaughter now, there is no harm." Another: "I did not realise, and slaughtered before stoning." — "Stone now, there is no harm." — Bukhari 1736',kn:'<b>With children:</b> you may throw the pebbles on behalf of a small child (agreed fiqh — Ibn Majah 3038 has a weak chain, its meaning is accepted) · keep them away from the Jamarat crush — one parent goes while the other waits with them · do the tawaf on the upper floor or at night.',steps:[
{id:'h10order',req:'sunnah',info:true,why:'He ﷺ stoned, then slaughtered, then shaved, then went to the House — and when people did it in another order he said "do it, no harm" (Bukhari 1736, 1723).',b:'The sunnah order: rami → hady → halq → tawaf al-ifadah',p:'Follow it if you can. If the group’s schedule or the crowds change the order, nothing is lost — the Prophet ﷺ was asked about every variation that day and answered "no harm".',miss:'Did them out of order? "No harm" (Bukhari 1736) — Shafi’is and Hanbalis say nothing is owed. Hanafis: a dam for shaving before the stoning, or (Qiran / Tamattu’) for sacrificing before the stoning or shaving before the hady; Malikis: a fidyah for shaving before the stoning, and a dam for a tawaf al-ifadah done before it. Mention it to your group leader so the rest of the day is sequenced right, and ask a scholar if your group follows the Hanafi school.'},
{id:'h10rami',req:'wajib',del:true,counter:'rami',why:'He ﷺ stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah on the Day of Nahr in the forenoon, seven pebbles, saying Allahu akbar with each (Tirmidhi 894, Bukhari 1750), and stopped the talbiyah with it (Bukhari 1685). After Aqabah he did not stop for dua (Bukhari 1753).',b:'Stone Jamrat al-Aqabah only — 7 pebbles, takbir with each',p:'Walk from Muzdalifah or your Mina camp to the big pillar (the one nearest Makkah, on the level of the bridge your group is assigned). One pebble at a time — never a handful — each with "Allahu akbar"; it must land inside the basin. Stop the talbiyah at the first pebble. Do not stop for dua here. Time: from sunrise until sunset; most scholars allow those who could not make it to stone at night (the Hanbali school has it done the next day after midday instead). The weak who left Muzdalifah early: Shafi’is and Hanbalis allow the stoning from after midnight (Bukhari 1679), Hanafis and Malikis only from Fajr — if unsure, wait for Fajr, which is valid for everyone. Too frail or in a wheelchair? Delegate it — see "Missed it?".',dua:{ar:'اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ',tl:'Allahu akbar',tr:'Allah is the Greatest — with every pebble.'},miss:'Not stoned today? Do it tonight (most scholars; the Hanbali school: tomorrow after midday) or, at the latest, tomorrow before that day’s three jamarat (today’s first, in order) — the stoning is made up. Shafi’is and Hanbalis: nothing more is owed; Hanafis: nothing if you stone before Fajr of the 11th, a dam if it waits until the next day; Malikis: a dam is owed for the delay. If the 13th passes with it undone, a dam is owed by all. Unable to go at all (illness, age, pregnancy, fear of the crush)? Appoint someone who has stoned for themselves to throw your seven — delegation for the one unable is permitted by all four schools (al-Mughni; the companions stoned on behalf of the children, Ibn Majah 3038 — weak chain, meaning accepted); Malikis add that the one unable still owes a dam. Tell your group leader / ask a scholar.'},
{id:'h10hady',req:'wajib',ht:['tamattu','qiran'],why:'"Whoever joins Umrah to Hajj must offer what hady is easy; whoever cannot, fasts three days during Hajj and seven when you return — ten in all" (Quran 2:196). The Prophet ﷺ slaughtered at Mina and said "all of Mina is a place of sacrifice" (Abu Dawud 1936).',b:'The hady — your sacrifice',v:{qiran:{p:'A Qarin owes a hady exactly like a Mutamatti’ (Quran 2:196 covers both by the majority view). Nearly every pilgrim pays it as an Adahi voucher through the package or the official Adahi platform; it is slaughtered on your behalf in Mina on the 10th or the days of tashriq — you do not need to be there. Keep the receipt. Once your time slot has passed, you may shave.'}},p:'Nearly every pilgrim pays it as an Adahi voucher through the package or the official Adahi platform; it is slaughtered on your behalf in Mina on the 10th or during the days of tashriq — you do not need to be there. Keep the receipt. Once your time slot has passed (or the voucher says so), you may shave. Buying a sheep yourself and attending is a beautiful sunnah if your group arranges it.',miss:'Cannot afford a hady? The Quran’s own alternative: fast three days during Hajj and seven days when you are home. Best the 6th–8th (Shafi’i) or the 7th–9th (Hanafi / Hanbali — the 9th is allowed for the one who owes this fast). Missed them? Malikis and Hanbalis allow the days of tashriq (Bukhari 1997–1998), as does al-Shafi’i’s old view; the relied-upon Shafi’i view does not, so the three days are made up later; Hanafis say the hady is then owed instead. Forgot to arrange it? It may be slaughtered until sunset on the 12th according to Hanafis, Malikis and Hanbalis, or the 13th according to many contemporary scholars (Shafi’is set no last day, though the 10th is best); after that most scholars say it is still owed and must be made up. Tell your group leader / ask a scholar.'},
{id:'h10udhiyah',req:'sunnah',ht:['ifrad'],why:'The hady of Quran 2:196 is for those who join Umrah to Hajj; a Mufrid owes none. An udhiyah on the day of Eid remains a sunnah for anyone who can, by most schools (the well-known Maliki view does not prescribe it for the pilgrim, and the Hanafi school makes it obligatory for a pilgrim who counts as resident and has the means).',b:'No hady is owed for Ifrad',p:'You carry no sacrifice debt. If you wish, an Adahi voucher as an udhiyah earns the reward of Eid (Hanafis: obligatory if you count as resident — 15+ days in Makkah — and have the means).',miss:'Nothing to make up — a Mufrid owes no hady. If a package insists you pay for one, tell your group leader / ask a scholar.'},
{id:'h10halq',req:'wajib',who:'m',pair:'hhair',why:'"O Allah, forgive those who shave their heads" — three times, and once for those who shorten (Bukhari 1728). "…with your heads shaved and [hair] shortened, not fearing" (Quran 48:27).',b:'Men: shave (halq) or trim all around (taqsir) — the first tahallul',p:'Shaving is the greater reward. Barbers crowd the Jamarat exits and the Aziziyah streets — use a licensed shop and a fresh blade. After the stoning and the cut (Shafi’is and Hanbalis: any two of stoning, cut and tawaf) you are in the first tahallul: everything is allowed again except intimacy and the marriage contract (Hanafis: intimacy only, since they permit the contract even in ihram; Malikis: and hunting; they also dislike perfume until tawaf al-ifadah, with nothing owed) — shower, normal clothes. Aishah (RA) perfumed the Prophet ﷺ at this point, before his tawaf (Bukhari 1539).',miss:'Changed into normal clothes before cutting? Put the ihram back on if you can and cut now. Whether a fidyah is owed differs: Shafi’is and Hanbalis excuse clothing worn forgetfully or unknowingly, but not cutting hair or nails; Hanafis require it regardless, and so do Malikis unless you had already stoned (for them the stoning alone opens the first tahallul); some Hanbalis and many contemporary scholars say nothing is owed for forgetting or not knowing (Bukhari 1536) — a fast of 3 days, feeding 6 poor, or a sheep (Quran 2:196; the amounts are from Bukhari 1814). Shaved before the hady or before the rami? "No harm" (Bukhari 1736) for Shafi’is and Hanbalis; Hanafis: a dam; Malikis: a fidyah for shaving before the rami. Left Makkah without ever cutting? Cut as soon as you remember, wherever you are — until then intimacy stays forbidden. Hanafis and Malikis say a dam is owed; Shafi’is and Hanbalis say nothing is owed for the delay. Tell your group leader / ask a scholar.'},
{id:'h10halqw',req:'wajib',who:'w',pair:'hhair',why:'"Shaving is not a duty laid on women; only clipping the hair is incumbent on them" (Abu Dawud 1984).',b:'Sisters: trim a fingertip’s length — the first tahallul',p:'Gather the hair and cut about 2 cm from the ends, somewhere private — in the tent, the ladies’ area or the hotel. A husband or mahram may cut it for you once he has exited his own ihram. From now on everything is allowed except intimacy and the marriage contract (Hanafis: intimacy only, since they permit the contract even in ihram; Malikis: and hunting; they also dislike perfume until tawaf al-ifadah).',miss:'Forgot to cut? Cut now. Your ordinary clothes were never a problem — women wear them in ihram — and the delay itself owes nothing if you cut by the 12th. If you did something else forbidden before cutting (perfume, cutting nails, niqab or gloves): Shafi’is and Hanbalis excuse perfume used forgetfully but not cutting nails; Hanafis require the fidyah regardless; Malikis owe nothing once you had stoned (the stoning alone opens their first tahallul); many contemporary scholars excuse all forgetting (Bukhari 1536) — a fast of 3 days, feeding 6 poor, or a sheep (Quran 2:196; the amounts are from Bukhari 1814). Left Makkah without cutting? Cut as soon as you remember. Hanafis and Malikis say a dam is owed; Shafi’is and Hanbalis say nothing is owed for the delay. Tell your group leader / ask a scholar.'},
{id:'h10ifadah',req:'rukn',counter:'ifadah',why:'"…then let them complete their rites, fulfil their vows and circle the Ancient House" (Quran 22:29). He ﷺ rode to the House and performed tawaf on the Day of Nahr, then prayed Dhuhr in Makkah (Muslim 1218). Safiyyah (RA) got her period after it, and he said "then she will not delay us" (Bukhari 1757).',b:'Tawaf al-ifadah — 7 rounds, with wudu, no raml, no idtiba’',p:'The pillar that completes your Hajj. Go to the Haram (today is the sunnah; any day of tashriq is fine — Hanafis: by sunset on the 12th — and later is still valid). Same 7 rounds as your Umrah tawaf, but in ordinary clothes and without the brisk walk (Hanafis and Shafi’is: raml in the first three if a sa’i follows) — then two rakahs and Zamzam. Open the counter — it will not touch your Umrah record. <b>Sisters on their period:</b> wait until you are pure, then do ghusl, tawaf and sa’i; your Hajj is intact and you remain in the first tahallul meanwhile. If your flight comes first, see "Missed it?".',miss:'Never done? The Hajj is not complete: intimacy stays forbidden until you do it — even if that means months later or a return trip; nothing else substitutes for it. Delayed past the 13th? Valid, and most scholars say nothing is owed (Hanafis: a dam once sunset on the 12th has passed, unless a period caused the delay; Malikis: a dam once Dhul Hijjah ends). A sister whose period will not end before the group leaves: scholars differ — some permit tawaf with precautions in genuine necessity; do not decide alone. Tell your group leader / ask a scholar.'},
{id:'h10sai',req:'rukn',why:'Aishah (RA): those who had assumed ihram for Umrah "performed tawaf round the Kaaba and between Safa and Marwah, then finished their ihram; after returning from Mina they performed another tawaf [between Safa and Marwah]. Those who had combined Hajj and Umrah performed only one" (Bukhari 1556). Tamattu’ = two sa’is; Qiran and Ifrad = one (Hanafis: two for a Qarin).',b:'The sa’i of Hajj — 7 laps, Safa to Marwah',v:{tamattu:{p:'Your Umrah sa’i was for the Umrah; this one is for the Hajj. Straight after tawaf al-ifadah, in ordinary clothes: the same Safa dhikr, 7 laps, finish on Marwah. Use the sa’i counter. With tawaf and sa’i done you are in the second tahallul — everything is allowed.'},qiran:{b:'The sa’i of Hajj — only if you have not already done it',p:'If you did sa’i after your arrival tawaf, you are done — one sa’i covers both for a Qarin by the majority view, and you are now in the second tahallul. If you did not, do it now after tawaf al-ifadah. Hanafis hold that a Qarin performs two sa’is (one for Umrah, one for Hajj): if you follow that school or your group leader says so, do the second one — an extra sa’i never harms.'},ifrad:{b:'The sa’i of Hajj — only if you have not already done it',p:'If you did sa’i after your arrival tawaf, nothing more is needed and you are now in the second tahallul. If you did not, do it now, straight after tawaf al-ifadah: Safa dhikr, 7 laps, finish on Marwah.'}},p:'Straight after tawaf al-ifadah: the Safa dhikr, 7 laps, finish on Marwah. With tawaf and sa’i done you are in the second tahallul — everything is allowed.',miss:'Forgot it or left it for later? Do it whenever you remember, even days later — it does not need ihram or a new tawaf, only to come after a tawaf you have already done (Malikis: do the sa’i straight after the tawaf; after a long gap repeat the tawaf first). Never done at all? It is a pillar for the majority (a wajib with a dam for Hanafis), so the Hajj stays incomplete until it is done. Tell your group leader / ask a scholar.'},
{id:'h10night',req:'wajib',why:'The Prophet ﷺ returned to Mina (Muslim 1308) and spent the nights of tashriq there (Aishah, Abu Dawud 1973); he exempted only the water-carriers of Zamzam and the herdsmen from the nights (Bukhari 1745, Abu Dawud 1975) — so for everyone else it is a duty (the majority view; Hanafis: a sunnah).',b:'Back to Mina — sleep there tonight',p:'After the Haram, return to your camp and spend the night (most of it) in Mina. Pray the prayers shortened (four rakahs if your school counts you a resident of Makkah — see the 8th), keep the takbir of tashriq going after each one, and rest — tomorrow’s stoning is after midday.',miss:'Spent the night in Makkah or Aziziyah with no excuse? Most scholars say a dam is owed if the nights are missed (some say one dam for all the nights; Hanafis treat the nights as sunnah with no dam). Illness, caring for the sick, or no space in the camp are valid excuses. Tell your group leader / ask a scholar.'}
]},
{id:'hd11',hd:11,ico:'🪨',title:'11 Dhul Hijjah · First day of tashriq',sub:'Three jamarat after midday · the night in Mina',hn:'"The days of Mina are three; whoever hastens in two days there is no sin on him, and whoever delays, there is no sin on him." — Tirmidhi 889 (cf. Quran 2:203)',steps:[
{id:'h11rami',req:'wajib',del:true,counter:'rami',why:'"We used to wait till the sun declined, then do the rami" (Ibn Umar, Bukhari 1746); "on the days after (Nahr) he stoned after the zenith of the sun" (Tirmidhi 894). He ﷺ stoned the first with seven pebbles saying takbir with each, went forward, faced the qibla and made a long dua; the same at the middle one; and did not stop after Aqabah (Bukhari 1753).',b:'After midday: small jamarah → middle → Aqabah, 7 pebbles each, dua after the first two',p:'Start with the small pillar (nearest Masjid al-Khayf), then the middle, then the big one towards Makkah — the order matters. One pebble at a time with "Allahu akbar". After the first and the second, step aside, face the qibla and make a long dua with your hands raised; after Aqabah, walk away. Time: from midday until sunset; most scholars allow those who cannot make it to stone during the night until Fajr (the Hanbali school has it done the next day after midday instead). Groups are given slots to avoid the crush — keep to yours.',dua:{ar:'اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ',tl:'Allahu akbar',tr:'Allah is the Greatest — with every pebble.'},miss:'Missed today? Stone it tomorrow before that day’s three, in order: today’s three first, then tomorrow’s — the stoning is made up. Shafi’is and Hanbalis: nothing more is owed; Hanafis and Malikis: a dam is owed for the delay. Stoned before midday? The majority count it as not done — repeat it after midday (Hanafis allow before midday only on the 13th; a few contemporary scholars permit it on the 12th for the weak in severe crowding — ask before relying on it). Unable to go? Delegate to someone who has stoned for themselves (permitted by all four schools — Ibn Majah 3038 has a weak chain, its meaning is accepted; Malikis add a dam for the one unable). If the 13th passes with any day undone, a dam is owed by all. Tell your group leader / ask a scholar.'},
{id:'h11worship',req:'sunnah',rep:'After every prayer · ends Fajr or Asr of the 13th, by school',why:'The days of tashriq are "days of eating, drinking and the remembrance of Allah" (Sahih Muslim); takbir after the prayers in these days is the practice of the companions.',b:'Takbir after every prayer · Quran · rest · dua',p:'Pray shortened in the camp or at Masjid al-Khayf — the Prophet ﷺ prayed two rakahs in Mina (Bukhari 1655); four rakahs if your school counts you a resident of Makkah (see the 8th) — say the takbir after each prayer, drink, eat well and sleep: your body is carrying you through five days of worship. Visit Masjid al-Khayf if it is near; call home.',dua:{ar:'اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ، اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ، لَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ، وَاللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ، اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ وَلِلَّهِ الْحَمْدُ',tl:'Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar, la ilaha illallah, wallahu akbar, Allahu akbar wa lillahil-hamd',tr:'Allah is the Greatest, Allah is the Greatest, there is no god but Allah; Allah is the Greatest, Allah is the Greatest, and to Allah belongs all praise.'}},
{id:'h11night',req:'wajib',why:'He ﷺ spent the nights of tashriq in Mina (Aishah, Abu Dawud 1973) and permitted only those with a duty elsewhere to miss them (Bukhari 1745).',b:'Sleep in Mina tonight',p:'Most of the night in the camp. If you went to the Haram for tawaf al-ifadah today, come back before you sleep.',miss:'Missed with no excuse? For this night alone the schools differ: the Malikis owe a dam, as do the Hanbalis in their relied-upon view (another Hanbali view: a mudd of food), the Shafi’is a mudd of food, the Hanafis nothing; missing all the nights owes a dam for the majority — see the night of the 10th. Excuses (illness, caring, no space) remove it. Tell your group leader / ask a scholar.'}
]},
{id:'hd12',hd:12,ico:'🚶',title:'12 Dhul Hijjah · Second day of tashriq',sub:'Three jamarat after midday · leave Mina before sunset, or stay for the 13th',hn:'"…whoever hastens in two days there is no sin on him." — Quran 2:203',steps:[
{id:'h12rami',req:'wajib',del:true,counter:'rami',why:'Exactly as the 11th: after the sun declines, small → middle → Aqabah, takbir with every pebble, dua after the first two (Bukhari 1746, 1753; Tirmidhi 894).',b:'After midday: small → middle → Aqabah, 7 each, dua after the first two',p:'Same sequence, same pace, same dua stops. If you are leaving Mina today, carry your bags to the buses first, then stone, then go — or stone and return for the bags, but watch the time. Pebbles: 21.',dua:{ar:'اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ',tl:'Allahu akbar',tr:'Allah is the Greatest — with every pebble.'},miss:'Missed or done before midday? Repeat after midday, or stone tomorrow in order (the 12th’s three, then the 13th’s) — the stoning is made up; Shafi’is and Hanbalis: nothing more is owed, Hanafis and Malikis: a dam for the delay. If the 13th passes with it undone, a dam is owed by all. Unable? Delegate (permitted by all four schools — Ibn Majah 3038 has a weak chain, its meaning is accepted; Malikis add a dam for the one unable). Tell your group leader / ask a scholar.'},
{id:'h12leave',req:'wajib',why:'"Whoever hastens in two days there is no sin on him, and whoever delays, there is no sin on him" (Quran 2:203). The Prophet ﷺ himself stayed for the 13th, remaining in Mina through the days of tashriq (Aishah, Abu Dawud 1973).',b:'Leaving today? Be out of Mina before the sun sets — otherwise stay and stone on the 13th',p:'Both choices are from the Quran: leave after today’s stoning (nafr al-awwal) or stay the extra night and stone again tomorrow (nafr ath-thani — what the Prophet ﷺ did, and the greater reward). If you choose to leave, your group must be outside Mina’s boundary before sunset. If the sun sets while you are still inside — the majority say you now stay the night and stone on the 13th (Hanafis: only if you are still there at Fajr). Being stuck in a departing bus inside the boundary is, for most scholars, excused.',miss:'Sun set while you were still in the camp with your bags, and you left anyway without stoning on the 13th? The majority say a dam is owed; some scholars excuse those who had already begun to leave and were only held up. Still in Mina now? Simply stay — the 13th’s stoning is after midday tomorrow. Tell your group leader / ask a scholar.'}
]},
{id:'hd13',hd:13,opt:'stay13',ico:'🌅',title:'13 Dhul Hijjah · Third day of tashriq',sub:'Only if you stayed · three jamarat after midday · then leave Mina',hn:'"…and whoever delays, there is no sin on him — for the one who fears Allah." — Quran 2:203',steps:[
{id:'h13night',req:'wajib',why:'Having stayed, the night of the 13th belongs to the nights of Mina, which the Prophet ﷺ spent there (Aishah, Abu Dawud 1973).',b:'The night of the 13th in Mina',p:'Same as the other nights — most of it in the camp.',miss:'Missed with no excuse? For a single night the schools differ: the Malikis owe a dam, as do the Hanbalis in their relied-upon view (another Hanbali view: a mudd of food), the Shafi’is a mudd of food, the Hanafis nothing (they count the nights as sunnah); missing all the nights owes a dam for the majority. Valid excuses remove it. Tell your group leader / ask a scholar.'},
{id:'h13rami',req:'wajib',del:true,counter:'rami',why:'The third day of stoning, after the sun declines (Bukhari 1746; Tirmidhi 894). Hanafis allow this last day’s rami before midday.',b:'After midday: small → middle → Aqabah, 7 each — then leave Mina',p:'Your last 21 pebbles, in order, with takbir and the two dua stops. Then collect your bags and leave Mina; the days of Mina are over. The Prophet ﷺ rested at al-Muhassab (al-Abtah) on the way into Makkah (Bukhari 1765) — a sunnah of convenience, not a rite.',dua:{ar:'اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ',tl:'Allahu akbar',tr:'Allah is the Greatest — with every pebble.'},miss:'Missed after midday and left? Stone before sunset if you can get back; after sunset of the 13th the days of stoning are over and a dam is owed for a day’s stoning left undone (for a pebble or two the schools differ). Unable? Delegate (permitted by all four schools — Ibn Majah 3038 has a weak chain, its meaning is accepted; Malikis add a dam for the one unable). Tell your group leader / ask a scholar.'}
]},
{id:'hdw',hd:null,ico:'👋',title:'Before you leave Makkah · Tawaf al-Wada',sub:'The last thing you do · 7 rounds · waived for sisters on their period',hn:'"The people were ordered to make the tawaf of the House the last thing — except the menstruating women, who were excused." — Bukhari 1755',steps:[
{id:'hwadaready',req:'sunnah',why:'"No one should depart until the last thing he does is the House" (Ibn Majah 3070) — so the shopping, the packing and the goodbyes come first.',b:'Finish everything else first — bags packed, shopping done',p:'The farewell tawaf must be your last act in Makkah. Settle the hotel, pack, buy the dates and Zamzam, then go to the Haram. Buying food for the road or waiting for your bus afterwards is fine; a long stay or another errand means repeating it (Hanafis: it still counts, though repeating it is preferred).',miss:'Did an errand afterwards and the bus has not left? Simply do the tawaf again. Already left? See the next step, and tell your group leader / ask a scholar.'},
{id:'hwada',req:'wajib',who:'m',pair:'hwada',counter:'wada',why:'The Prophet ﷺ ordered it as the last act before leaving (Bukhari 1755, Muslim). He ﷺ himself did it at night, after Isha and a short sleep at al-Muhassab, before setting out for Madinah (Anas, Bukhari 1764).',b:'Tawaf al-wada — 7 rounds in ordinary clothes, then leave',p:'With wudu, 7 rounds, no raml, no idtiba’, no sa’i; two rakahs after it if you can, a last drink of Zamzam, a last dua at the door — and go. If you leave tawaf al-ifadah to the day you depart, it counts as the farewell tawaf too for the Malikis and Hanbalis (and Ibn Baz and Ibn Uthaymin); the Shafi’is still require a separate farewell tawaf — ask your group leader. Not required if you live in Makkah. Going to Madinah next? Do it when you leave Makkah, not after Madinah. Jeddah counts as leaving.',miss:'Left Makkah without it? If you are still close (Shafi’is and Hanbalis: under about 80 km, the distance for shortening prayers; Hanafis: before passing the miqat), go back and do it — nothing is owed. If you are already far away or home, the majority say a dam is owed (a sheep slaughtered in the Haram, which a trusted person there can arrange); Malikis hold it is a sunnah with nothing owed. Tell your group leader / ask a scholar.'},
{id:'hwadaw',req:'wajib',who:'w',pair:'hwada',counter:'wada',why:'The same command — "except the menstruating women, who were excused" (Bukhari 1755). When Safiyyah (RA) got her period after tawaf al-ifadah, the Prophet ﷺ said: "then depart" (Bukhari 1771).',b:'Sisters: tawaf al-wada if you are pure — waived if you are on your period',p:'If you are pure: 7 rounds in ordinary clothes, no sa’i, then leave as your last act. If your period (or postnatal bleeding) has started and you have already done tawaf al-ifadah: you owe nothing and may leave straight away — say your goodbyes to the House with dua from the courtyard or the gate, without entering the prayer hall. If you have NOT yet done tawaf al-ifadah, that is a different matter — see its step.',miss:'Left without it while pure? If still close, go back and do it; if far away, the majority say a dam is owed (Malikis: nothing). Left while menstruating? Nothing is owed at all. Tell your group leader / ask a scholar.'}
]}];

/* Two-tap answers for the days of Hajj — rendered under the Hajj rites and in the rami counter’s Problem? sheet. */
var TROUBLE_HAJJ=[
{id:'hmissrami',t:'🪨 I missed the stoning time',b:'<p>You have not lost the Hajj. <strong>Stone it the next day, in order:</strong> yesterday’s jamarat first (small → middle → Aqabah; only Aqabah if you missed the 10th), then today’s — all after midday. The stoning is made up: Shafi’is and Hanbalis say nothing more is owed; Hanafis and Malikis say a <strong>dam</strong> (a sheep, slaughtered and given to the poor of the Haram) is owed for the delay. If the 13th passes with any stoning undone, a dam is owed by all. If you cannot physically go, <strong>delegate</strong> it to someone who has already stoned for themselves (permitted by all four schools — Ibn Majah 3038 has a weak chain, its meaning is accepted; Malikis add a dam for the one unable). Tell your group leader / ask a scholar.</p>'},
{id:'hmenses',t:'🩸 My period started before tawaf al-ifadah',b:'<p>Your Hajj is intact. Arafah, Muzdalifah, the stoning, the trim — all valid without purity. Only tawaf al-ifadah (and the sa’i after it) waits: carry on with every other rite, and after the stoning and the trim on the 10th stay in the first tahallul; keep up the dhikr and dua from the courtyards, and do ghusl, tawaf and sa’i when you are pure — even on the 13th or later. Aishah (RA), whose period came before her tawaf, was told: "Do all that the pilgrims do, except the tawaf of the House until you are pure" (Bukhari 305, 1650). <strong>The farewell tawaf is waived</strong> for you if you are still on your period when you leave (Bukhari 1755). If the group’s flight comes before purity, scholars differ — some permit tawaf with precautions in genuine necessity; do not decide alone at the airport: tell your group leader / ask a scholar now, not on the last day.</p>'},
{id:'hweak',t:'🧓 I am too weak for Muzdalifah / the stoning',b:'<p>Islam planned for you. <strong>Muzdalifah:</strong> leave after midnight with your carer — the Prophet ﷺ sent the weak of his family ahead (Bukhari 1678); nothing is owed. The time of the stoning then differs: Shafi’is and Hanbalis allow it from after midnight (Asma stoned before Fajr, Bukhari 1679); Hanafis and Malikis hold it is valid only from Fajr — follow your group’s school, and if unsure wait for Fajr, which is valid for everyone. <strong>Stoning:</strong> appoint someone who has stoned for themselves to throw on your behalf (permitted by all four schools — Ibn Majah 3038 has a weak chain, its meaning is accepted; Malikis add a dam for the one unable) — your group leader can arrange it; or go at night (allowed by most scholars), when the bridge is quieter. <strong>Tawaf and sa’i:</strong> wheelchairs are valid — the Prophet ﷺ performed tawaf mounted (Bukhari 1607); wheelchairs (free or rented at official points around the Haram) and electric carts (upper floors and roof) are available — ask the Haram staff. Illness is not a shortcoming in your Hajj. Tell your group leader / ask a scholar.</p>'},
{id:'hlost',ck:CK,t:'🧭 I lost my group in Mina',b:'<p>Mina is a grid of numbered camps: find your <strong>maktab / camp number</strong> on your wristband or card and show it to any scout (kashaf), security officer or the guides for lost pilgrims at the street corners — they will walk you there. No wristband? Say your country and group name; the Hajj mission tents of each country are signposted. Phones struggle in Mina: agree a meeting time and place with your family every morning. Emergency: <strong>911</strong>; Ministry of Hajj care line <strong>1966</strong>. Never leave Mina to look for your group in Makkah. Tell your group leader / ask a scholar.</p>'},
{id:'hforbidden',t:'🙈 I did something forbidden in ihram',b:'<p>Stop the act, remove the cause, put the ihram back on and continue — the Hajj stands. <strong>Forgetting or not knowing:</strong> Shafi’is and Hanbalis excuse perfume and clothing but not cutting hair or nails; Hanafis and Malikis require the fidyah regardless; some Hanbalis and many contemporary scholars say nothing is owed (Bukhari 1536 — a man in a perfumed cloak was simply told to remove it). A <strong>deliberate</strong> violation (perfume, cutting hair or nails, men covering the head or wearing stitched clothes) needs the fidyah by all once it reaches each school’s threshold: fast three days, or feed six poor people, or slaughter a sheep (Quran 2:196; the amounts are from the hadith of Ka’b ibn Ujrah, Bukhari 1814) — Hanafis give this choice only when there was an excuse; without one they require a sheep. Below the threshold less is owed — for hair, a mudd of food per hair under three hairs (Shafi’is and Hanbalis) or a charity under a quarter of the head (Hanafis). <strong>Intimacy</strong> is different: before the first tahallul (Hanafis: before Arafah) it spoils the Hajj — you still complete it and repeat it another year, and a camel (badanah) is owed (Malikis: a hady; Hanafis: a sheep). After Arafah but before shaving, Hanafis say the Hajj stands and a camel is owed (after shaving, a sheep). Tell your group leader / ask a scholar whether a fidyah applies to you.</p>'},
{id:'hlatearafah',t:'🏔️ I reached Arafah after sunset / missed the standing',b:'<p>Arrived any time before Fajr on the 10th? Your <strong>wuquf is valid</strong> — "whoever comes [to Arafah] on the night of Jam’, before Fajr, has caught the Hajj" (Tirmidhi 889); go on to Muzdalifah with the group. Did not reach Arafah at all before Fajr? The Hajj is missed for this year: you exit ihram by the acts of Umrah (tawaf, sa’i, cutting the hair), a hady is owed according to the majority (Hanafis: none), and the Hajj is repeated another year — the obligation still stands. Tell your group leader / ask a scholar before you do anything.</p>'},
{id:'hwrongorder',t:'🔀 I did the 10th in the wrong order',b:'<p>Shaved before the sacrifice, stoned after shaving, tawaf before stoning — the Prophet ﷺ was asked about every one of these on the Day of Nahr and answered <strong>"do it, there is no harm"</strong> (Bukhari 1736, 1722; Muslim 1306). Just complete what is left. Shafi’is and Hanbalis say nothing is owed; Hanafis: a dam for shaving before the stoning, or (Qiran / Tamattu’) for sacrificing before the stoning or shaving before the hady; Malikis: a fidyah for shaving before the stoning, and a dam for a tawaf al-ifadah done before it. Tell your group leader so the rest of the day is sequenced right, and ask a scholar if your group follows the Hanafi school.</p>'},
{id:'hnohady',t:'🐑 I cannot afford the hady',b:'<p>The Quran gives the alternative itself: <strong>fast three days during Hajj and seven when you return home — ten in all</strong> (Quran 2:196). Best the 6th–8th (Shafi’i) or the 7th–9th (Hanafi / Hanbali — the 9th is allowed for the one who owes this fast; a pilgrim with nothing to make up does not fast Arafah). Missed them? Malikis and Hanbalis allow the days of tashriq (Bukhari 1997–1998), as does al-Shafi’i’s old view; the relied-upon Shafi’i view does not, so the three days are made up later; Hanafis say the hady is then owed instead. Many groups can also sponsor a voucher — ask. Tell your group leader / ask a scholar.</p>'},
{id:'hleftwada',t:'👋 I left Makkah without the farewell tawaf',b:'<p>Still near Makkah (Shafi’is and Hanbalis: under about 80 km, the distance for shortening prayers; Hanafis: before passing the miqat)? <strong>Go back and do it</strong> — nothing is owed. Already far or home? The majority say a dam is owed (a sheep in the Haram — arranged through someone trustworthy there); Malikis consider the farewell tawaf a sunnah with nothing owed. Sisters who left while menstruating owe nothing at all (Bukhari 1755). Tell your group leader / ask a scholar.</p>'}];

/* 6.6 (G2 #15) · pebble problems for the rami Problem? sheet — VERBATIM from the verified arabic-phrases pack (checked 2026-11-09; rulings keep their school labels and
   "Tell your group leader / ask a scholar"; al-Muwatta is cited without a number). Keep in sync with HAJJ_RITES h9npebbles / h10rami–h13rami and TROUBLE_HAJJ. a = the counter action the card offers. */
var RAMI_PROB=[
{id:"rami-not-in-basin",t:"The pebble did not land in the basin",a:"keep",b:"<p>Throw another pebble in its place. Only a pebble that lands inside the basin counts, so do not add the one that missed to your count.</p><p>It does not have to hit the wall: the wall only marks the target. A pebble that lands in the basin counts even if it bounces out again (Ibn Baz; Ibn Uthaymin; al-Fawzan). One that falls short does not count (al-Mughni). One that hits something hard or someone’s clothing and then drops into the basin counts (al-Mughni; the Malikis likewise, Mukhtasar Khalil).</p><p>Not sure it went in? If it most likely went in, it counts (al-Mughni). If you cannot tell, throw another: al-Shafi’i said it does not count until you know it landed in place (al-Umm), and another pebble settles it.</p><p>Hanafis: a pebble that lands just beside the jamrah also counts; one that lands far from it does not (al-Hidayah). Aim into the basin and every school counts it.</p>"},
{id:"rami-two-at-once",t:"Threw two or more at once",a:"minus",b:"<p>They count as one. Take the extra off your count and throw the rest one at a time, with “Allahu akbar” for each. The Prophet ﷺ threw seven pebbles one after the other, saying takbir with each (Muslim 1218; Bukhari 1753). This is the view of Ahmad, Malik, al-Shafi’i and the Hanafis; Ata’ held otherwise (al-Mughni).</p>"},
{id:"rami-lost-count",t:"Not sure how many you have thrown",a:"set",b:"<p>Go by the number you are sure of and throw the rest. Unsure whether it was five or six? Count five and throw two more (Ibn Baz). The Prophet ﷺ gave this rule for doubt in prayer: “let him cast aside the doubt and build on what he is sure of” (Muslim 571), and al-Mughni applies the same rule to counting the rounds of tawaf.</p><p>A doubt that only comes after you have finished is not acted on (Ibn Baz; al-Mughni gives the same rule for tawaf).</p><p>Found out later that a pebble was certainly missed? The schools differ. According to al-Mughni, Imam Ahmad’s apparent view is that nothing is owed for one or two pebbles. The evidence given is Sa’d ibn Abi Waqqas (RA): coming back from the Hajj with the Prophet ﷺ, some said “I threw seven” and others “six”, and none faulted the other (al-Nasa’i 3077; al-Albani: chain sahih; Zubair Ali Zai: da’if). Ibn Baz also excused a doubt about a single pebble. The Malikis owe a dam even for one pebble; the Shafi’is a dam for three, and a mudd of food for each pebble below that; the Hanafis half a sa’ of food for each pebble, up to the value of a dam (al-Mughni). While the days of stoning last it can still be made up, and the order of the jamarat matters, so tell your group leader / ask a scholar.</p>"},
{id:"rami-dropped-pebble",t:"Dropped a pebble, or ran out",a:"keep",b:"<p>Use another one. A pebble that fell from your hand was not thrown, so your count stays where it was. Any small stone counts, whether you picked it up at Muzdalifah, in Mina or on the road (al-Mughni reports no disagreement on this). The only question is a pebble that has already been thrown (below).</p><p>In the crowd, do not bend down to search the floor. Step out of the flow first, or use a spare: carrying a few extra pebbles helps.</p><p>Avoid pebbles from inside the basin, because they have already been thrown. The Hanbalis say a used pebble does not count (al-Mughni). The Shafi’is count it (al-Mughni). The Hanafis and the Malikis count it but dislike it: Malik told Ibn al-Qasim, who had lost a pebble and thrown one of the Jamarat pebbles instead, “That is disliked, and I see nothing owed by you” (al-Mudawwanah). The Hanafis also dislike taking pebbles from beside the jamrah (al-Hidayah). A pebble from anywhere else counts for everyone.</p>"},
{id:"rami-pebble-size",t:"How big should the pebbles be?",b:"<p>Small: about the size of a chickpea or a date stone, the “pebbles for flicking” (hasa al-khadhf). When Ibn Abbas (RA) picked them up for him, the Prophet ﷺ said: “Pebbles like these. And beware of going to extremes in religion” (al-Nasa’i 3057, sahih per al-Albani). Jabir (RA) saw him stone the Jamrah with pebbles like hasa al-khadhf (Muslim 1299).</p><p>Too big or too small? The Malikis, the Shafi’is and the preferred Hanafi view count a bigger stone, though it is disliked. The Hanbalis say a very small or a big stone does not count: theirs should be “bigger than a chickpea and smaller than a hazelnut”. A middle size counts for everyone, and a big stone can hurt someone in the crowd (al-Mughni).</p><p>Throw stones, not shoes, wood or other things. The Hanafis also accept clay or anything else of the earth; the other schools accept only stone.</p><p>Pick up small ones rather than breaking a big stone, which can send a chip into your face (al-Mughni). There is no need to wash them (the Prophet ﷺ is not reported to have done so; some scholars recommend it).</p>"},
{id:"rami-delegate",t:"Can someone else stone for me?",b:"<p>Yes, if you cannot do it yourself, or only with real hardship: illness, old age, pregnancy, blindness or weakness, or a mother with small children and no one to mind them. A guardian also stones for a small child. Delegation is permitted by all four schools (al-Mughni); the Malikis add that the one unable still owes a dam (al-Muwatta; Mukhtasar Khalil). The Companions stoned on behalf of the children (Ibn Majah 3038: weak chain, its meaning is accepted).</p><p>Healthy, and only worried about the crowd? Go at a quieter time, such as the night (allowed by most scholars; the Hanbali school: the next day after midday), rather than delegate (Ibn Baz).</p><p>Who stones: a trustworthy person who is on Hajj this year (the Standing Committee). They begin with themselves: at each jamrah they throw seven for themselves, then seven for you, then move on. They do not need to finish all three for themselves first (Ibn Baz; Ibn Uthaymin).</p><p>If you can, follow the time and say the takbir where you are: Imam Ahmad liked the person to be present if able, and Malik said the sick person says the takbir in his lodging at the time (al-Mughni; al-Muwatta).</p><p>Got better while the days of stoning remain? Shafi’is: no need to repeat, though repeating is recommended (al-Nawawi; al-Shinqiti). Malik: repeat what was thrown for you, and a dam is owed (al-Muwatta). Tell your group leader / ask a scholar.</p>"},
{id:"rami-night-late",t:"Stoning at night, or late",b:"<p>The times: on the 10th, Jamrat al-Aqabah from sunrise until sunset (for the weak who left Muzdalifah early, Shafi’is and Hanbalis allow it from after midnight, Hanafis and Malikis only from Fajr). On the 11th, 12th and 13th, from midday until sunset. The Prophet ﷺ stoned on the 10th in the forenoon and on the later days after the sun had passed its height (Muslim 1299).</p><p>At night: most scholars allow it for those who could not stone by day: the Hanafis (until Fajr, except on the 13th), the Shafi’is, and the Malikis, who count the night as make-up time with a dam for the delay (Mukhtasar Khalil). The Hanbali school does not count stoning at night: it is done the next day after midday, the missed day first (al-Shinqiti, Adwa’ al-Bayan).</p><p>Missed a whole day? Stone it the next day after midday, in order: the missed day’s three first (only Aqabah if it was the 10th), then that day’s. Shafi’is and Hanbalis: nothing more is owed. Hanafis and Malikis: a dam for the delay (Hanafis: nothing for the 10th if you stone before Fajr of the 11th).</p><p>Stoned before midday on the 11th, 12th or 13th? The majority count it as not done: repeat it after midday (Hanafis allow it before midday on the 13th).</p><p>After sunset on the 13th the days of stoning are over, and a dam is owed by all for a day’s stoning left undone (for a pebble or two the schools differ: see “Not sure how many you have thrown”). Tell your group leader / ask a scholar.</p>"}];

/* 6.6 (G2 #16) · the Hajj dhikr chip of the tasbih — info lines VERBATIM from the verified arabic-phrases pack; the Arabic and transliteration are read from DUAS (byte-identical, checked by the pack). */
var TB_HAJJ={
talb:{t:"The talbiyah",ar:"لَبَّيْكَ اللَّهُمَّ لَبَّيْكَ، لَبَّيْكَ لَا شَرِيكَ لَكَ لَبَّيْكَ، إِنَّ الْحَمْدَ وَالنِّعْمَةَ لَكَ وَالْمُلْكَ، لَا شَرِيكَ لَكَ",b:"<p>Here I am, O Allah, here I am. Here I am, You have no partner, here I am. All praise, favour and sovereignty are Yours. You have no partner. (Bukhari 1549)</p><p>Say it often from the moment you enter ihram: men aloud, women softly. In Hajj, keep it going until you stone Jamrat al-Aqabah on the 10th: the Prophet ﷺ kept on saying it until he stoned the Jamrah (Bukhari 1685). Most scholars stop at the first pebble; the Malikis stop at Arafah after midday on the 9th (al-Muwatta). In Umrah, the majority stop when they begin tawaf. Use the counter only to keep your own tally.</p>"},
arafah:{t:"The dua of the Day of Arafah",ar:"لَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ وَحْدَهُ لَا شَرِيكَ لَهُ، لَهُ الْمُلْكُ وَلَهُ الْحَمْدُ وَهُوَ عَلَىٰ كُلِّ شَيْءٍ قَدِيرٌ",b:"<p>None has the right to be worshipped but Allah alone, without partner. His is the dominion and His is the praise, and He is able to do all things.</p><p>The Prophet ﷺ said: “The best of supplication is the supplication of the Day of Arafah, and the best of what I and the prophets before me have said is: la ilaha illallahu wahdahu la sharika lah…” (Tirmidhi 3585). Grade: al-Albani, hasan; al-Tirmidhi called it hasan gharib and noted that one narrator is not strong; Zubair Ali Zai, da’if. Malik reports a shorter wording as a mursal report (al-Muwatta).</p><p>Say it often on the Day of Arafah, with your own duas in between, until sunset. These same words said 100 times in a day have their own reward (Bukhari 3293; Muslim 2691).</p>"},
takbir:{t:"The takbir of the days of Mina",ar:"اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ، اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ، لَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ، وَاللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ، اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ وَلِلَّهِ الْحَمْدُ",b:"<p>Allah is the Greatest, Allah is the Greatest, there is no god but Allah; Allah is the Greatest, Allah is the Greatest, and to Allah belongs all praise.</p><p>This is not a hadith of the Prophet ﷺ: it is the takbir that the Companion Ibn Mas’ud (RA) said in the days of tashriq, recorded by Ibn Abi Shaybah in al-Musannaf with a chain its editor grades sahih. No wording is fixed from the Prophet ﷺ, so other forms are also fine.</p><p>Say it after the prayers and at any time in these days. Ibn Umar (RA) would say the takbir in Mina after the prayers, on his bed, in his tent, where he sat and as he walked (reported by al-Bukhari in a chapter heading, without its chain).</p><p>The takbir after the prayers starts and ends at different times in each school. For the pilgrim: Malikis and Shafi’is, from Dhuhr on the 10th to Fajr on the 13th; Hanbalis (and Ibn Baz and Ibn Uthaymin), from Dhuhr on the 10th to Asr on the 13th; Hanafis, from Fajr on the 9th to Asr on the 13th. Follow your group’s school.</p>"}};

/* 6.6 (G2 #53) · today's rulings on each day of Hajj — VERBATIM from the verified heat-rulings pack (checked 2026-11-09). d = the days in the pack's "use" field ("9n" = the Muzdalifah night).
   rule-mina-prayers, rule-d9-arafah-prayers, rule-d9n-muzdalifah-prayers, rule-d10-hady-udhiyah and rule-d10-13-no-fasting mirror HAJJ_RITES h8mina, h9salah, h9nsalah, h10hady / h10udhiyah: change them together.
   No madhhab picker and no post-prayer takbir reminders (gonogo.md): the school differences are shown exactly as written. */
var HAJJ_RULINGS=[
{id:"rule-d9-no-fast",d:[9],t:"Pilgrims do not fast the Day of Arafah",b:"<p>The famous fast of Arafah is for those not on Hajj. At Arafah the Prophet ﷺ did not fast: when people disagreed about it, Umm al-Fadl (RA) sent him a cup of milk and he drank it on his camel (Bukhari 1988; Muslim 1123). Maymunah (RA) reported the same, with the people watching (Bukhari 1989; Muslim 1124). Ibn Umar (RA) said that neither the Prophet ﷺ nor Abu Bakr, Umar or Uthman fasted it on Hajj (Tirmidhi 751). At-Tirmidhi wrote that most scholars prefer that the pilgrim not fast at Arafah, to have strength for dua, though some did fast it (Tirmidhi 750).</p><p>The schools: the Malikis, Shafi’is and Hanbalis say the pilgrim at Arafah should not fast it. Breaking the fast is better, and some of them call fasting there disliked. The Hanafis allow it if it does not weaken you for the standing and the duas, and dislike it if it does. In mid-May heat, eat, drink and keep your strength for dua. (A pilgrim fasting the three days in place of a hady may make the 9th one of them according to the Hanafis and Hanbalis: see the hady.)</p><p>Family at home: fasting the Day of Arafah is a sunnah; the Prophet ﷺ said he hoped from Allah that it expiates the year before and the year after (Muslim 1162).</p>"},
{id:"rule-d9-way-talbiyah",d:[9],t:"On the way to Arafah: talbiyah, and takbir too",b:"<p>As you move from Mina to Arafah on the morning of the 9th, keep up the talbiyah. Saying the takbir is also fine: Anas (RA) said that on this journey with the Prophet ﷺ some said the talbiyah and some said the takbir, and no one objected to either (Bukhari 970, 1659).</p>"},
{id:"rule-d9-arafah-prayers",d:[9],t:"Arafah: Dhuhr and Asr together, shortened",b:"<p>At Arafah the Prophet ﷺ prayed Dhuhr and then Asr, both at the time of Dhuhr, with one adhan and two iqamahs and nothing in between (Jabir, Muslim 1218), each shortened to two rakahs. Salim told al-Hajjaj that the sunnah is to pray early, just after midday, that day, and Ibn Umar (RA) said he had spoken the truth (Bukhari 1662). This is the practice of the majority. Abu Hanifa allowed this combining only for one praying behind the Hajj imam, so many Hanafi groups pray each prayer at its own time in their tents. A pilgrim whose school counts them a resident of Makkah prays four rakahs each (see Mina’s prayers); in the relied-upon Shafi’i view such a resident also prays each at its own time, since the combining is tied to travel. Prayed them separately, or at Asr time? Both prayers are valid and nothing is owed.</p>"},
{id:"rule-d9n-muzdalifah-prayers",d:["9n"],t:"Muzdalifah: Maghrib and Isha together on arrival",b:"<p>Do not stop for Maghrib on the way out of Arafah: when asked, the Prophet ﷺ said, “The prayer is ahead of you” (Bukhari 1667). On reaching Muzdalifah he prayed Maghrib and Isha together, with one adhan and two iqamahs and no voluntary prayer between them (Muslim 1218; Bukhari 1673, 1674). Maghrib stays three rakahs; Isha is shortened to two (four if your school counts you a resident of Makkah; in the relied-upon Shafi’i view such a resident also prays Maghrib and Isha each at its own time). If you fear you will arrive only after midnight, many scholars (including the Saudi fatwa bodies) say pray both on the way before midnight; the four schools still count Isha as on time until Fajr when there is a need. Never let Fajr come without praying them. Prayed Maghrib before reaching Muzdalifah? For the majority it is valid; the Hanafis say it must be prayed again at Muzdalifah before Fajr.</p>"},
{id:"rule-mina-prayers",d:[8, 10, 11, 12, 13],t:"Mina: shorten, but do not combine",b:"<p>In Mina the Prophet ﷺ prayed each prayer at its own time, shortening the four-rakah prayers to two (Bukhari 1653, 1655, 1656; Muslim 1218). Maghrib and Fajr are never shortened.</p><p>Who shortens: the Malikis shorten in Mina and Arafah regardless, as a rite of Hajj, as do many Saudi scholars today. The Hanafis (15+ days in Makkah before the 8th) count you a resident, and you pray four rakahs. So do the Shafi’is after 4 full days in Makkah (not counting the days of arrival and departure), unless you will set off for home after the rites without another 4 full days in Makkah: then you shorten from the moment you leave for Mina (al-Majmu’ 8/88). The Hanbalis shorten unless you will stay on in Makkah for more than 4 days after the Hajj. Follow your group’s school, and ask your group leader.</p>"},
{id:"rule-d10-no-eid-prayer",d:[10],t:"Pilgrims are not asked to pray Eid",b:"<p>Pilgrims at Mina do not hold the Eid prayer. On the Day of Nahr the Prophet ﷺ stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah, sacrificed and rode on to the House (Jabir, Muslim 1218), and gave a sermon that day (Bukhari 1739, 1741); Eid fell during his Hajj and he did not pray it, as the Hanbali jurists point out. None of the four schools asks the pilgrim to hold it, to lighten a day full of rites: the Hanafis say it is not held at Mina; the Malikis, that it is not recommended for the pilgrim; the Hanbalis make settled residence a condition of the Eid prayer; the Shafi’is say the pilgrim does not pray it in congregation, but may pray it alone, and some of them call that better. Spend the morning on the stoning, the sacrifice, the shave and tawaf al-ifadah, with the takbir on your tongue.</p>"},
{id:"rule-d10-hady-udhiyah",d:[10],t:"Hady and udhiyah: not the same",b:"<p>The hady is the sacrifice of Hajj. It is owed by a pilgrim doing Tamattu’ or Qiran (Quran 2:196) and is slaughtered within the Haram: “all of Mina is a place of sacrifice” (Abu Dawud 1936). A pilgrim doing Ifrad owes none. Cannot afford it? Fast three days during the Hajj and seven when you are home (Quran 2:196). Its time: until sunset on the 12th according to the Hanafis, Malikis and Hanbalis, or the 13th according to many contemporary scholars; the Shafi’is set no last day, though the 10th is best.</p><p>The udhiyah is the Eid sacrifice that Muslims everywhere offer. For the pilgrim it is a sunnah according to most schools; the well-known Maliki view does not prescribe it for the pilgrim, and the Hanafi school makes it obligatory for a pilgrim who counts as resident and has the means.</p>"},
{id:"rule-d10-13-no-fasting",d:[10, 11, 12, 13],t:"No fasting on Eid or the days of tashriq",b:"<p>No one fasts on the Day of Eid (Bukhari 1990). The days of tashriq, the 11th, 12th and 13th, are “days of eating, drinking and remembrance of Allah” (Muslim 1141). Fasting them was allowed only for the pilgrim who could not afford a hady (Bukhari 1997, 1998), and even that is disputed: the Malikis, the Hanbalis and al-Shafi’i’s old view allow that pilgrim to fast them; the Hanafis and the relied-upon Shafi’i view do not. Eat well and drink plenty: you need it in the heat.</p>"},
{id:"rule-takbir-words",d:[9, 10, 11, 12, 13],t:"The takbir of these days: the words",ar:"اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ، اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ، لَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا اللَّهُ، وَاللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ، اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ وَلِلَّهِ الْحَمْدُ",tl:"Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar, la ilaha illallah, wallahu akbar, Allahu akbar wa lillahil-hamd",b:"<p>A common wording: “Allah is the Greatest, Allah is the Greatest, there is no god but Allah; Allah is the Greatest, Allah is the Greatest, and to Allah belongs all praise.” This is not a hadith of the Prophet ﷺ: it is the takbir that the Companion Ibn Mas’ud (RA) said in the days of tashriq, recorded by Ibn Abi Shaybah in al-Musannaf with a chain its editor grades sahih. No single wording is fixed from the Prophet ﷺ, so there is room: the Hanafis prefer this form, al-Shafi’i preferred starting with three takbirs, and Ibn Abbas (RA) used another wording.</p><p>Besides after the prayers, you may say it at any time in these days. Umar (RA) said it in his tent in Mina, the people took it up, and Mina rang with takbir; Ibn Umar (RA) said it after the prayers, on his bed, in his tent, where he sat and as he walked (reported by al-Bukhari in a chapter heading, without its chain).</p>"},
{id:"rule-takbir-times",d:[9, 10, 11, 12, 13],t:"The takbir after prayers: when it starts and ends",b:"<p>Say it after each obligatory prayer in these days. Its start and end come from Companion reports that differ, so the schools differ:</p><p>Malikis and Shafi’is: for the pilgrim, from Dhuhr on the 10th to Fajr on the 13th.</p><p>Hanbalis (and Ibn Baz and Ibn Uthaymin): for the pilgrim, from Dhuhr on the 10th to Asr on the 13th.</p><p>Why Dhuhr on the 10th: the pilgrim says the talbiyah until the stoning that morning, and Dhuhr is the first prayer after it.</p><p>Hanafis: once after every obligatory prayer from Fajr on the 9th to Asr on the 13th, travellers and those praying alone included.</p><p>Not on Hajj? Many scholars start it at Fajr on the Day of Arafah, as reported from Ali and Ibn Abbas (RA); Ibn Mas’ud (RA) said it from Fajr on the Day of Arafah to Asr on the Day of Nahr. Follow your group’s school. The general takbir is allowed at any time in these days.</p>"}];

