/* ── 6.9 (G5 #51 / #52 / #54 / #55 / #56 / #21) · the verified content packs, verbatim: Ramadan Umrah, the heat plan, wayfinding (Jamarat, camp, getting around, boundaries).
   Generated from enh/packs/*.verified.json (checked 2026-11-09) — titles, text and Arabic are never edited by hand. `on` is the date each item was checked. ── */
var RAM_PACK=[
{"id":"ram-umrah-virtue","t":"An Umrah in Ramadan","x":"A woman of the Ansar had not been able to join the Hajj, so the Prophet ﷺ told her: “When Ramadan comes, perform Umrah in it, for an Umrah in Ramadan is equal to a Hajj” (Bukhari 1782; Muslim 1256). Another narration says “equal to a Hajj with me” (Bukhari 1863; Muslim 1256). Scholars explain that this refers to its reward: it does not replace the obligatory Hajj.","ar":"فَإِذَا كَانَ رَمَضَانُ اعْتَمِرِي فِيهِ، فَإِنَّ عُمْرَةً فِي رَمَضَانَ حَجَّةٌ","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"ram-qadr","t":"Laylat al-Qadr: seek it in the odd nights","x":"Laylat al-Qadr “is better than a thousand months” (Quran 97:3). The Prophet ﷺ said: “Seek Laylat al-Qadr in the odd nights of the last ten nights of Ramadan” (Bukhari 2017). He also said: “Whoever stands in prayer on Laylat al-Qadr out of faith and hoping for reward will have his past sins forgiven” (Bukhari 1901). An Islamic night begins at sunset, so the odd nights are the nights before the 21st, 23rd, 25th, 27th and 29th days of Ramadan.","ar":"تَحَرَّوْا لَيْلَةَ الْقَدْرِ فِي الْوِتْرِ مِنَ الْعَشْرِ الأَوَاخِرِ مِنْ رَمَضَانَ","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"ram-qadr-dua","t":"The dua for Laylat al-Qadr","x":"Aishah asked the Prophet ﷺ what she should say if she knew which night was Laylat al-Qadr. He said: “Say: O Allah, You are Pardoning and You love to pardon, so pardon me.” (Ibn Majah 3850 and Tirmidhi 3513. Al-Tirmidhi called it hasan sahih and al-Albani graded it sahih, though some graders weaken its chain.) Many printed copies of al-Tirmidhi add the word “karim” (Generous); al-Albani found it in none of the early sources and judged it a copyists’ addition, so the shorter wording is given here.","ar":"اللَّهُمَّ إِنَّكَ عَفُوٌّ تُحِبُّ الْعَفْوَ فَاعْفُ عَنِّي","tl":"Allahumma innaka ’afuwwun tuhibbul-’afwa fa’fu ’anni","tr":"O Allah, You are Pardoning and You love to pardon, so pardon me.","s":"Ibn Majah 3850 · Tirmidhi 3513","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"ram-last-ten","t":"How the Prophet ﷺ spent the last ten nights","x":"When the last ten nights began, the Prophet ﷺ would tighten his waist-wrapper, pray through the night and wake his family (Bukhari 2024). He used to observe i’tikaf in the last ten days of Ramadan until Allah took him (Bukhari 2026).","ar":"كَانَ النَّبِيُّ صلى الله عليه وسلم إِذَا دَخَلَ الْعَشْرُ شَدَّ مِئْزَرَهُ، وَأَحْيَا لَيْلَهُ، وَأَيْقَظَ أَهْلَهُ","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"ram-crowds","t":"The busiest nights of the year","x":"Ramadan is the peak season for Umrah, and the crowds build from the first days: the Grand Mosque authority reported more than 904,000 people performing Umrah on 4 Ramadan 1447 (21 February 2026) and expected the numbers to rise in the last ten nights. Many hope for Laylat al-Qadr on the 27th night, as the Companion Ubayy ibn Ka’b held (Muslim 762), so that night draws some of the largest crowds of the year: for the 26th day and 27th night of Ramadan 1446 (March 2025), the authority reported about 3.4 million worshippers and about 800,000 Umrah pilgrims. The Prophet ﷺ told us to seek it in all the odd nights of the last ten (Bukhari 2017), so give every one of them your best. Arrive long before the prayer and follow staff to open areas.","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"ram-taraweeh","t":"Taraweeh and tahajjud in the Haram","x":"The Prophet ﷺ said: “Whoever prays at night in Ramadan out of faith and hoping for reward will have his past sins forgiven” (Bukhari 2009), and: “Whoever prays with the imam until he finishes, a whole night of prayer is written for him” (Tirmidhi 806, which al-Tirmidhi called hasan sahih and al-Albani graded sahih). In the Grand Mosque, taraweeh is prayed in congregation after Isha. In the last ten nights a second congregational night prayer, tahajjud, is held late in the night. Times and imams are announced each year.","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"ram-fasting","t":"The reward of your fast","x":"The Prophet ﷺ said: “Whoever fasts Ramadan out of faith and hoping for reward will have his past sins forgiven” (Bukhari 38). And: “The fasting person has two joys: one when he breaks his fast, and one when he meets his Lord” (Bukhari 1904).","ar":"مَنْ صَامَ رَمَضَانَ إِيمَانًا وَاحْتِسَابًا غُفِرَ لَهُ مَا تَقَدَّمَ مِنْ ذَنْبِهِ","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"ram-give-iftar","t":"Give iftar","x":"The Prophet ﷺ said: “Whoever gives a fasting person food to break his fast will have the same reward as him, without the fasting person’s reward being reduced at all” (Tirmidhi 807, which al-Tirmidhi called hasan sahih and al-Albani graded sahih). Inside the two Harams, iftar is served through an official programme: in Ramadan 1447 (2026), donors booked iftar spreads on the website of the mosques’ authority, paid through the Ehsan platform, and approved caterers served the meals. Look for the authority’s announcement for Ramadan 1448.","ar":"مَنْ فَطَّرَ صَائِمًا كَانَ لَهُ مِثْلُ أَجْرِهِ غَيْرَ أَنَّهُ لاَ يَنْقُصُ مِنْ أَجْرِ الصَّائِمِ شَيْئًا","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"ram-iftar-haram","t":"Iftar in the Haram","x":"At sunset the Grand Mosque breaks its fast together. Charities and volunteers set out iftar in the mosque and its courtyards, commonly dates and Zamzam, and the spreads are cleared quickly so the rows can form for Maghrib. Spreads inside are run by permit holders with an approved food list and must not block walkways or emergency routes, so sit where staff direct you. The sunnah is to break the fast as soon as the sun has set (Bukhari 1954, 1957), with fresh dates, or dry dates, or a few sips of water (Abu Dawud 2356, graded hasan sahih by al-Albani).","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"ram-iftar-dua","t":"After breaking your fast","x":"When the Prophet ﷺ broke his fast, he would say: “The thirst has gone, the veins are moistened, and the reward is certain, if Allah wills.” (Abu Dawud 2357, graded hasan by al-Albani)","ar":"ذَهَبَ الظَّمَأُ وَابْتَلَّتِ الْعُرُوقُ وَثَبَتَ الْأَجْرُ إِنْ شَاءَ اللَّهُ","tl":"Dhahabaz-zama’u wabtallatil-’uruqu wa thabatal-ajru in sha’ Allah","tr":"The thirst has gone, the veins are moistened, and the reward is certain, if Allah wills.","s":"Abu Dawud 2357","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"ram-suhoor","t":"Suhoor, and when to stop","x":"The Prophet ﷺ said: “Take suhoor, for there is blessing in suhoor” (Bukhari 1923). You may eat and drink until dawn, when the fast begins (Quran 2:187). The Grand Mosque gives two adhans for Fajr: an earlier first call, then the Fajr adhan itself. In the Prophet’s ﷺ time Bilal also called before dawn, and the Prophet ﷺ said: “Eat and drink until Ibn Umm Maktum calls the adhan” (Bukhari 617). When the app shows calculated prayer times (marked ±2 min), Fajr can be a minute or two off the official timetable, so stop eating a few minutes early.","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"ram-excused","t":"Who may skip a fast","x":"Allah allows the sick and the traveller not to fast and to make up the same number of days later (Quran 2:184–185). A woman does not fast during her period and makes the days up later (Bukhari 1951; Muslim 335). For those too old to fast, Ibn Abbas explained that they feed a poor person for each day instead (Bukhari 4505).","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"ram-traveller","t":"Fasting while travelling, and during your stay","x":"On the journey you may fast or not. The Prophet ﷺ told a Companion, “Fast if you wish, and do not fast if you wish” (Bukhari 1943), and called it “a concession from Allah: whoever takes it has done well, and whoever prefers to fast has no sin” (Muslim 1121). Any day you do not fast is made up later. Once you are staying in Makkah or Madinah, scholars differ. Most (the Maliki, Shafi’i and Hanbali schools) count you a resident, who must fast, once you intend to stay in one city for about four days or more: the Shafi’is and Malikis count four full days, not counting the days you arrive and leave; the Hanbalis, more than four days. The Hanafi school sets the line at an intended stay of 15 days or more. Other scholars hold that the concession lasts as long as you are travelling, however long you stay. Ask a scholar you trust, or your group’s guide, which view to follow.","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"ram-nusuk","t":"Umrah permits in Ramadan","x":"Your Umrah needs a free Umrah permit (a date-and-time slot) in the Nusuk app, in Ramadan as at any time of year. Nusuk allows one active permit at a time: you can book another once it expires, or cancel it before its start time. Praying in the Grand Mosque needs no permit. In past Ramadans the Ministry of Hajj and Umrah has limited repeat Umrahs to ease crowding: in 2023 it said each person could perform Umrah only once in Ramadan, in 2024 it said no permit would be issued for a second Umrah in Ramadan, and in 2025 it encouraged pilgrims to perform Umrah only once in the last ten days. Check Nusuk for the rule in Ramadan 1448.","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"ram-itikaf","t":"I’tikaf in the Haram","x":"I’tikaf inside the two Harams needs advance registration with the mosques’ authority, and places are limited. In Ramadan 1447 (2026), registration opened online on 3 Ramadan through the authority’s e-services (with a Nafath log-in for Saudi citizens and residents); applications were accepted by capacity and confirmed by text message, and i’tikaf was set for 20 Ramadan to the end of the month. In 2025 applicants had to be at least 18 and Saudi citizens or residents. Rules for Ramadan 1448 have not been announced yet, so check the authority’s official channels before you plan on it.","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"ram-season-1448","t":"Umrah visa dates for Ramadan 1448","x":"For the 1448 season the Ministry of Hajj and Umrah set these dates: 1 Shawwal 1448 (9 March 2027) is the last day for issuing Umrah visas, 15 Shawwal (23 March 2027) is the last day for Umrah pilgrims to enter the Kingdom, and 30 Shawwal (7 April 2027) is the last day to leave. If you travel on another type of visa, check its own rules.","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"ram-energy","t":"Fasting, tawaf and sleep","x":"A full Umrah (seven circuits of tawaf and seven laps of sa’i) is a long walk on an empty stomach, and Makkah is warm in Ramadan 1448 (February to early March 2027): long-term average daytime highs are about 33°C in February and 35°C in March. Consider doing your Umrah after iftar, once you have eaten and drunk. The Ministry of Health advises drinking at least 10 cups of water spread between iftar and suhoor, not all at once at suhoor, limiting tea and coffee, and keeping out of the sun in the daytime. If you feel dizzy or confused or cannot stand, drink water at once, ideally an oral rehydration solution, as the Ministry advises: the sick are excused from fasting and make the day up later (Quran 2:184). In the last ten nights the prayers run late, so plan to rest after Fajr or in the afternoon.","on":"2026-11-09"}
];
var HEAT_PACK=[
{"id":"heat-why-1448","t":"Why heat needs a plan this Hajj","x":"Hajj 1448 falls in mid-May 2027. By the Umm al-Qura calendar, 8 Dhul Hijjah is Friday 14 May and the Day of Arafah is Saturday 15 May; the official announcement can move this by a day. In Makkah the average May high is about 42°C, and 49°C has been recorded in May. The Saudi Ministry of Hajj and Umrah lists where heatstroke is common: tawaf at noon, sa’i when it is crowded and hot, Arafah at noon, and Mina, especially the slaughter areas and the Jamarat, because of the long walk and the crowding at the stoning. Plan your days around these places and hours.","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"heat-hours","t":"Keep out of the sun from 10 am to 4 pm","x":"Saudi Arabia’s Ministry of Health has told pilgrims to keep out of direct sun between 10 am and 4 pm, to use an umbrella in those hours, to avoid walking on hot surfaces, and not to climb hills or mountains on the Day of Arafah (2025). On the Day of Arafah in 2026 it asked pilgrims to stay inside their camps until 4 pm. Your standing at Arafah counts inside your tent: there is no need to climb Jabal ar-Rahmah or to stand in the sun. Rest after each rite, and follow the Ministry’s advice for 2027 when it is published.","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"heat-jamarat-slot","t":"Jamarat: keep to your group’s time","x":"Groups are given set times for the Jamarat: keep to yours. The Ministry of Hajj and Umrah asks pilgrims to follow the official schedules to cut the risk of heat exhaustion and sunstroke. A later time costs you nothing: on the 11th, 12th and 13th the stoning time starts after midday (about 12:17 pm in Mina in mid-May 2027) and lasts until sunset (about 6:52 pm), and most scholars allow the night for those who could not stone by day (the Hanbali school: the next day after midday). On the 10th the Prophet ﷺ stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah in the forenoon, and on the later days after midday (Muslim 1299). Take water and an umbrella, and get back into the shade straight after.","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"heat-exhaustion","t":"Heat exhaustion: cool down now","x":"Signs: headache; dizziness or fainting; heavy sweating with pale, clammy skin; nausea or vomiting; cramps; tiredness or weakness; intense thirst.\nWhat to do: get into shade or air conditioning. Loosen or remove extra clothing. Spray or sponge the skin with cool water, especially the head and neck, and fan it; cold packs wrapped in cloth on the neck or armpits help. Drink cold water, or oral rehydration salts mixed with water.\nStay with the person: they should feel better within about 30 minutes. If they are not better by then, or they vomit, or they get worse, call 911. Heat exhaustion can turn into heatstroke.","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"heat-stroke","t":"Heatstroke is an emergency: call 911","x":"Signs: body temperature of 40°C or more; hot, red skin that may be dry or still sweaty; fast heartbeat and fast breathing; confusion, odd behaviour, or seeing and hearing things that are not there; a seizure; collapse or loss of consciousness.\nWhat to do: call 911 first (or 997, the Saudi Red Crescent). Then cool the person hard while you wait: move them to shade or air conditioning, remove outer clothing, pour or spray cool water over them, especially the head and neck, and fan them; put ice or cold packs on the neck and armpits if you have them. Do not try to make someone drink if they are confused, drowsy or unconscious. If they become unconscious, lay them on their side in the recovery position and check their breathing; start CPR if there is no sign of life.\nFor medical advice by phone, the Ministry of Health line 937 answers 24 hours a day (in the 2025 Hajj season, in seven languages).","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"heat-hydration","t":"Drink before you feel thirsty","x":"Drink water often through the day, even when you are not thirsty. Water is best; avoid sugary and caffeinated drinks. If you have been sweating heavily, or feel weak or dizzy, oral rehydration salts or an isotonic drink help replace the salt you lose in sweat. Keep a filled bottle with you at Arafah, in Mina, at Muzdalifah and on the walk to the Jamarat. If a doctor limits how much you drink (for example for heart or kidney disease), ask them before you travel how to manage in the heat.","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"heat-ihram-shade","t":"Shade in ihram: what men may use","x":"Men in ihram must not cover the head. The Prophet ﷺ forbade the man in ihram the turban and the hooded cloak (Bukhari 1542), and scholars agree this rules out a cap, hat or ghutra, or a towel or cloth laid on the head. Shade that does not touch the head is different.\nTents, buildings and trees: allowed by consensus. On the Day of Arafah the Prophet ﷺ rested in a tent pitched for him at Namirah (Muslim 1218).\nA hand-held umbrella: allowed by most scholars (the Hanafis, the Shafi’is, one narration from Imam Ahmad, and some Maliki authorities such as Khalil). When the Prophet ﷺ stoned Jamrat al-Aqabah, Usamah and Bilal (RA) were with him: one led his camel and the other held up a cloth over his head to shade him from the heat (Muslim 1298). Some jurists disliked or forbade it, but there is no consensus against it.\nUmbrella hats held on by a band round the head: better avoided. Most schools forbid tying a band round the head in ihram; Egypt’s Dar al-Ifta allows such a hat only when nothing else is available, with a small charity if it is worn for a whole day, and advises a hand-held umbrella instead.\nWomen cover their heads as usual, and may use an umbrella.","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"heat-wet-head","t":"Cool off with water: ihram allows it","x":"Pouring water over your head is allowed in ihram. Asked how the Prophet ﷺ washed his head in ihram, Abu Ayyub (RA) had water poured over his own head, passed his hands over it from front to back and said, “This is how I saw him do it” (Bukhari 1840). So pour or spray water on your head, face, neck and arms whenever you feel hot. Men: wet the head directly; do not leave a wet towel or cloth resting on it. If you use wipes or soap, choose unscented ones: perfume is not allowed in ihram.","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"heat-kit","t":"Your heat kit for Arafah and Mina","x":"A light umbrella. A refillable water bottle. Oral rehydration sachets. Sunglasses. A small spray bottle for water. Loose, light-coloured clothing (sisters) and a spare ihram (brothers). Your usual medicines, with a written list of them and their doses. The numbers 911, 997 and 937 saved in your phone and written on paper.","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"heat-companions","t":"Watch each other","x":"Heat is hardest on older people and on anyone with heart disease, diabetes, kidney or lung conditions, and some medicines add to the risk. Confusion is one of the signs of heatstroke, so the person may not ask for help: check on your companions through the day. See a doctor before you travel. Tell your group leader or group doctor early if anyone feels unwell; in the 2025 season the Ministry of Health equipped field units near the holy sites with beds for heat exhaustion.","on":"2026-11-09"}
];
var WAY_PACK=[
{"id":"jam-order","t":"The three jamarat, in order","x":"Walking from Mina towards Makkah, you meet them in this order:\n1. The small jamrah (as-Sughra, also called the first): the first one after Masjid al-Khayf.\n2. The middle jamrah (al-Wusta).\n3. Jamrat al-Aqabah (al-Kubra, the big one): at the Makkah end of Mina, the last one you reach.\nOn the 10th you stone only Jamrat al-Aqabah. On the 11th and 12th (and the 13th if you stay) you stone all three, seven pebbles each, in this order: small, middle, then Aqabah.","ar":"الجمرة الصغرى (الأولى) · الجمرة الوسطى · جمرة العقبة (الكبرى)","tl":"al-Jamrah as-Sughra (al-Ula) · al-Jamrah al-Wusta · Jamrat al-Aqabah (al-Kubra)","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"jam-wall-basin","t":"What you aim at: the wall and the basin","x":"Each jamrah is a basin with a wall standing in its middle. The Ministry of Hajj’s guide says: throw seven pebbles, one at a time, and say \"Allahu akbar\" with each; aim at the wall in front of you so that the pebble lands in the basin; come from the least crowded side, and take care not to hit anyone. Scholars such as Ibn Baz and Ibn Uthaymeen say a throw counts when the pebble lands in the basin, whether or not it hits the wall.","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"jam-history","t":"From pillars to walls","x":"Until 2004 the jamarat were tall pillars. By the Hajj of early 2005 they had been replaced by long, thick walls, a bigger target. In 2005 the basins around them were reshaped from round to oval to ease the movement of pilgrims. After the 2006 Hajj it was decided to replace the old bridge, and today’s multi-level Jamarat facility was built in stages over the following years.","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"jam-facility","t":"The Jamarat facility","x":"Today the jamarat stand inside a five-level building, about 950 m long and 80 m wide, with 11 entrances and 12 exits and separate routes in and out. Your group is given a route, a level and a time: keep to them, and follow the signs and the security staff. Do not stop in the walkway: to make dua after the first two jamarat, step aside out of the path. After Jamrat al-Aqabah, do not stop; move on towards the exit.","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"jam-aqabah-stance","t":"The 10th: where to stand at Jamrat al-Aqabah","x":"Ibn Mas’ud (RA) reached the big jamrah and stood with the House (the Ka’bah) on his left and Mina on his right. He threw seven pebbles and said: \"This is how the one to whom Surat al-Baqarah was revealed ﷺ threw\" (Bukhari 1748; Muslim 1296). Stand like this if you easily can. In a crowd, follow the Ministry of Hajj’s advice and come from the least crowded side. Scholars such as Ibn Baz and Ibn Uthaymeen make it a condition that each pebble lands in the basin. Do not stop for dua after this jamrah.","ar":"جَعَلَ الْبَيْتَ عَنْ يَسَارِهِ، وَمِنًى عَنْ يَمِينِهِ، وَرَمَى بِسَبْعٍ، وَقَالَ هَكَذَا رَمَى الَّذِي أُنْزِلَتْ عَلَيْهِ سُورَةُ الْبَقَرَةِ صلى الله عليه وسلم","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"jam-dua-spots","t":"11th to 13th: where to pause for dua","x":"After the small jamrah, move a little ahead and out of the path. Face the qibla, raise your hands and make a long dua. After the middle jamrah, step aside to the left, out of the path, face the qibla and make dua again. After Jamrat al-Aqabah, do not stop: walk on. Ibn Umar (RA) did this and said: \"This is how I saw the Prophet ﷺ do it\" (Bukhari 1751; also Bukhari 1753). Today, make your dua where you do not block anyone.","ar":"ثُمَّ تَقَدَّمَ أَمَامَهَا فَوَقَفَ مُسْتَقْبِلَ الْقِبْلَةِ رَافِعًا يَدَيْهِ يَدْعُو","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"jam-getting-there","t":"Getting to the Jamarat","x":"Your group decides how and when you go. Some groups take the Mashaer train, whose last station is on the 4th level of the Jamarat facility, and then walk a short way. Others go by buses set aside for particular groups, or on foot; if you walk, the Ministry advises a sun umbrella and comfortable shoes. Golf carts carry the elderly and those who find walking hard, especially up to the Jamarat Bridge. Go at your group’s stoning time. In 1447 (2026) the Ministry’s instructions to Hajj companies said pilgrims going to the stoning should not bring children or luggage, or use wheelchairs; check this season’s rules with your company. If you are elderly, weak, ill or pregnant, or fear harm, you may appoint another pilgrim to stone for you (tawkil). Your proxy stones for himself first, then for you.","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"camp-card","t":"Your Mina camp card","x":"Roads and camps in Mina look very alike. The Ministry of Hajj advises you to save these on your phone and to carry proof of them with you:\n- Area number, street and camp number\n- Your camp’s gate\n- The nearest building or landmark\n- The nearest train station number, if your group uses the Mashaer train\n- Your group supervisor’s name and phone number\nAlso save the camp’s location in your phone’s map app, and keep your Nusuk card and pilgrim bracelet on you at all times.","ar":"رقم المنطقة · الشارع · رقم المخيم · البوابة · أقرب مبنى · رقم محطة القطار · مشرف الحملة","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"camp-finding-way","t":"Finding your way back in Mina","x":"- Before you leave the camp, tell your supervisor, go with a companion and take your phone.\n- Note the gate you leave by and a building or landmark you can see from it.\n- To get back, use the location you saved in your phone’s map app.\n- Miqah cannot show a verified map of Mina’s camps. Ask your camp guide how your camp’s signs and numbers read before you need them.\n- If you are lost, show your Nusuk card to security staff, a scout or a Nusuk Care point. The Ministry calls the card the main way to identify you and get you help. Scouts run guidance points for lost pilgrims across Mina and walk them back to their camp.","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"help-numbers","t":"Numbers to call","x":"911: emergencies and security.\n937: medical advice and questions.\n1966: the Ministry of Hajj and Umrah’s unified number for questions and complaints, open 24 hours (inside Saudi Arabia).\n920002814: the Ministry’s contact centre for callers outside the Kingdom.\nNusuk Care centres in Makkah, Madinah and the holy sites also help lost pilgrims.","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"go-haramain","t":"Haramain High Speed Railway","x":"Five stations: Makkah (in al-Rusaifah), Jeddah (al-Sulaymaniyah), King Abdulaziz International Airport (linked to Terminal 1), King Abdullah Economic City, and Madinah (in Knowledge Economic City). The Makkah and Madinah stations are not next to the Harams: in a straight line they are about 4 km from the Ka’bah and about 9 km from the Prophet’s Mosque, so plan a taxi or bus for the last part.\nOfficial 2026 journey times: about 45 minutes from the airport to Makkah, about 1 hour 45 minutes from the airport to Madinah, and about 2¼ hours from Makkah to Madinah. Some trains take longer, so check the time of your own train, and book only on the official Haramain website or app.\nGoing from Madinah to Makkah for Umrah or Hajj? The train does not stop at the miqat. Put on ihram before boarding, and make the intention before the train passes the miqat.","on":"2026-11-09","src":[["pep.gov.sa","https://pep.gov.sa/en/news/962"],["sar.com.sa","https://www.sar.com.sa/"]]},
{"id":"go-mashaer-metro","t":"The Mashaer metro (Holy Sites train)","x":"Runs only in the Hajj season, between Arafah, Muzdalifah and Mina. It has nine stations, three at each site; the last one is on the 4th level of the Jamarat facility. It does not run to the Haram or to the hotel districts of Makkah. In Hajj 1447 (2026) it ran for seven days, from 7 Dhul Hijjah to the end of the days of tashriq.\nNot every pilgrim rides it: the train is used by the groups assigned to it. If your group is one of them, your company tells you your station and times. Keep your train bracelet on your wrist, do not take luggage on board, do not sit or lie down in the stations, and follow your group’s train schedule.","on":"2026-11-09","src":[["sar.com.sa","https://www.sar.com.sa/featured/almashaertrain/"],["haj.gov.sa","https://haj.gov.sa/-/media/Project/HAJJ/PDF-and-FILES/Awareness-Guides/Arafah-Guide/French/FR-Arafah-Guide.pdf"]]},
{"id":"go-makkah-bus","t":"Makkah Bus","x":"Makkah Bus is the city’s public bus network, launched by the Royal Commission for Makkah City and Holy Sites. In January 2026 it had 12 routes and about 430 stops. Routes link stations near the Haram with the neighbourhoods, the Haramain train station, Masjid Aisha (Tan’eem) and the Hira Cultural District. Buy tickets in the Makkah Bus app or website, at ticket machines, or at sales points. Some routes have been changed in past Hajj seasons, so check the app for your route and times before you rely on it.","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"go-madinah-bus","t":"Madinah Buses","x":"Madinah Bus is the city’s public bus network, overseen by the Madinah Region Development Authority. In 2025 its routes included one between the airport and the Prophet’s Mosque, and others serving the Haramain train station, the miqat and Masjid Quba. In the 2026 Hajj season it also ran extra shuttles to the Prophet’s Mosque and Masjid Quba. Routes change, so check routes, times and how to pay in the official Madinah Bus channels before you travel.","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"go-taxi","t":"Taxis and ride-hailing apps","x":"Use a licensed taxi, or a ride-hailing app licensed by the Transport General Authority; in 2024 the licensed apps included Careem and Uber. Licensed taxis show the driver’s and the operating company’s names. At airports, follow the signs for the official taxi rank or your app’s pick-up point. The Authority warns visitors not to accept rides from unlicensed drivers who approach them.","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"go-walking","t":"Walking between the holy sites","x":"Your Hajj company moves you between the sites on a fixed plan, by bus or train, and some groups walk to the Jamarat. The Ministry of Hajj tells pilgrims to travel with their group by the designated transport, for example from Arafah to Muzdalifah, and in 2025 it asked pilgrims not to walk between the sites at the restricted times. If your group’s plan does include walking, these are rough guides at a steady 3 to 5 km an hour. Walking routes are longer than these distances, and crowds and heat slow you down, so allow more time:\n- Haram to the Jamarat: about 5 km in a straight line; camps at the far (Muzdalifah) end of Mina are about 8 km or more. 1 to 3 hours.\n- Mina to Arafah: about 10 km, more between camps far apart. 2 to 4 hours or more.\n- Muzdalifah to the Jamarat: about 6 km from the middle of Muzdalifah; less from its Mina side, more from its Arafah side. 1 to 2 hours.\n- Arafah to Muzdalifah: about 6 to 7 km. 1 to 2½ hours.\nIf you walk, the Ministry advises a sun umbrella and comfortable shoes.","on":"2026-11-09","src":[["haj.gov.sa","https://haj.gov.sa/-/media/Project/HAJJ/PDF-and-FILES/Awareness-Guides/Arafah-Guide/French/FR-Arafah-Guide.pdf"]]},
{"id":"bound-arafah","t":"Am I inside Arafah?","x":"The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Hajj is Arafah\" (Tirmidhi 889), and: \"I stood here, and all of Arafah is a place of standing\" (Muslim 1218). So make sure you are inside Arafah for the standing, and check with your eyes, not with a phone. Signboards mark Arafah’s boundary; read them to see which side is Arafah. The valley of Uranah, on Arafah’s western edge, is not part of Arafah, and the western (front) part of Masjid Namirah stands in it. The Ministry of Hajj advises staying in your camp, because Arafah’s roads look alike and it is easy to get lost, and praying there rather than going to Masjid Namirah unless necessary, to avoid getting lost or heatstroke. If you are unsure, ask your group leader or an official.","ar":"وَقَفْتُ هَا هُنَا وَعَرَفَةُ كُلُّهَا مَوْقِفٌ","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"bound-muzdalifah-mina","t":"Muzdalifah and Mina: where they begin and end","x":"Muzdalifah lies between Arafah and Mina. Its eastern edge, towards Arafah, is the pass between the two mountains of al-Ma’zimayn. Its western edge is the eastern bank of Wadi Muhassir, the valley that separates it from Mina; the valley itself is not part of Muzdalifah. Mina runs west from Wadi Muhassir up to Jamrat al-Aqabah. The Prophet ﷺ said: \"I stood here, and all of Jam’ (Muzdalifah) is a place of standing\" (Muslim 1218). Reports in the Muwatta and Ibn Majah tell pilgrims to keep out of the hollow of Muhassir. Where your bus stops, look for the boundary signs, and ask your group leader if you are unsure.","ar":"وَقَفْتُ هَا هُنَا وَجَمْعٌ كُلُّهَا مَوْقِفٌ","on":"2026-11-09"},
{"id":"gps-save-camp","t":"Save your camp's location","x":"When you reach your camp in Mina or Arafah, stand at the camp gate and save the spot. Miqah keeps it on your phone, offline, and shows the straight-line distance and direction back to it. It only shows where you saved the pin. It cannot tell you whether a place is inside Mina, Muzdalifah or Arafah. Phone GPS is typically accurate to about 5 metres under open sky, and worse near buildings, bridges and other obstructions. Use it with your camp card, not instead of it.","on":"2026-11-09"}
];
